{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-DRAFT.md; cat collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.astra-review.stdout.log; rg -n 'context-upstream-form|kernel-entries|registered-definition|yin.module/derivations|Gate S|H3-6' test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj yin/vm/linker.cljc test/yang/latte_oracle/context.edn\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-DRAFT.md; cat collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.astra-review.stdout.log; rg -n 'context-upstream-form|kernel-entries|registered-definition|yin.module/derivations|Gate S|H3-6' test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj yin/vm/linker.cljc test/yang/latte_oracle/context.edn\"","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-11-05 03:40:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-05 10:40:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n# RULING DRAFT — attribution-as-query: the executed-arity rule attributes through the unitary record (DRAFT — pending gpt-6-astra review and owner sign-off)\n\nProvenance of this draft: the owner's axiom-2/unitarity observation (\"the yang compiler pipeline is a transformation over dao.stream and preserves unitarity\") applied to the last uncredited arity. The owner's Datalog reframing is the ruling's core: the attribution question is a query over datoms, not a string-matching convention. Status: DRAFT — gpt-6-astra reviews before owner sign-off.\n\n## The problem (recorded)\n\nThe kernel gate credits an upstream function-arity when an execution trace names it at the exact arity in both C lanes. The matcher keys on NAME+ARITY STRINGS. For `latte-kernel.defenv/registered-definition?`, this fails structurally: the guest's adaptation (context.edn) has arities 2 and 3 (the guest world has no ambient environment), the 2-arg body self-delegates to the 3-arg form, and upstream's `/1` (ambient) and `/2` (explicit) both correspond to guest executions summarized as `[registered-definition? 2]`/`[3]`. The name+arity string destroys the provenance the pipeline preserved. A global \"+1\" trace-table mapping was proposed and REJECTED (arity- and call-origin-unsafe). Result: 164/165 credited, the gate honestly `:blocked` on one arity, H3-6 (which requires 165/165) blocked behind an owner ruling.\n\n## The architectural facts this ruling is grounded in (verified today)\n\n1. **The linker already records derivations** (yin/vm/linker.cljc §8.1): module manifests carry `:yin.module/derivations` — addresses of derivation records per format (`:yin.semantic/code`, `:yin.debruijn.code`, `:yin.debruijn.register`) with `:yin.module/contracts` revision strings. The derivation-record mechanism exists; it is currently unused for the oracle's adaptation module.\n2. **The adaptation is pipeline data**: `test/yang/latte_oracle/context.edn` is EDN — the guest-side stand-in functions (explicit `kernel` context, outcome tuples, `:latte/kind` maps) compiled through yang.clojure into guest UAST. Its host-side pre-transformation (`context-upstream-form`, a walk/postwalk binding-rewrite) runs OUTSIDE the record as harness code.\n3. **The correspondence is not a datom**: \"this context.edn function adapts `latte_kernel.defenv/registered-definition?`\" lives in the harness's manifest entries and prose — not as a derivation datom.\n4. **The gate's attribution is string matching**: `kernel-entries` (latte_oracle_test.clj:1442) from harness manifest entries + host `top-forms` arities; traces matched by name+arity.\n\n## The ruling (seven rules)\n\n1. **Attribution is defined as a query over datoms.** The executed-arity rule's satisfying form: a trace attributes to an upstream function-arity when the query — trace → guest callable → its UAST/module datoms → derivation datom → upstream ns/name, plus the declared call-shape correspondence — resolves to exactly that arity. Name+arity string matching is demoted to an optimization, valid only where the query is degenerate (1:1).\n2. **The adaptation becomes a stream citizen.** The context module is compiled through the standard linker with a manifest carrying `:yin.module/derivations` naming its upstream (`latte_kernel.defenv`, `latte_kernel.norm`, `latte_kernel.nbe`, `latte_kernel.presyntax`, `latte_kernel.syntax`, `latte_kernel.unparser`) — the correspondence becomes a datom, not prose. The host-side `context-upstream-form` pre-transformation is either recorded as part of the derivation (a declared transform with its own revision) or moved into the pipeline; host-side invisible rewriting of to-be-recorded source is not unitary and ends.\n3. **The ambient correspondence is a declared, disjoint pattern.** A `[registered-definition? 2]`-shape execution with the adapter's ambient empty-context attributes to upstream `/1`; with a caller-supplied context, to upstream `/2`. The patterns are disjoint by argument provenance, which the record carries (the fixture's declared call shape + the stored bodies) — call-origin preserved by construction, the property the withdrawn +1 mapping could not give.\n4. **Gate S v1's frozen wording gets a versioned attribution amendment.** The executed-arity rule (\"the exact mapped arity in both C lanes\") is satisfied by address/derivation-resolved attribution per rules 1-3; \"mapped\" is defined by the derivation datoms, not by name equality. One specification bump, no other rule changes; admission denominator unchanged.\n5. **`registered-definition?/1` credits under the query** — the F2 fixture's declared ambient-shape execution, verified entering the mapped body in both C lanes. The gate then reads 165/165 and H3-6 proceeds.\n6. **No weakening rides along.** The D2 packet machinery, the cell/authentic? vocabulary, the evidence-walk bounds, and the refusal pins are untouched. The query ADDS attribution power; it removes nothing.\n7. **H3-6's audit runs the query.** The closure audit reconstructs 165/165 by executing the attribution query over the final tree's datoms — \"by address or by a query over its datoms,\" the invariant's own phrasing.\n\n## Honest scoping (what exists vs what must land)\n\n- EXISTS: the linker §8.1 derivation-record mechanism; context.edn as pipeline data; the manifest entries naming upstream ns/name; the execution records.\n- MUST LAND: (a) the adaptation module's manifest derivation records (naming its upstream); (b) the declared-transform recording for context-upstream-form (or its absorption into the pipeline); (c) the attribution query implementation (dao.space.query/q over module datoms + execution records); (d) the Gate S attribution amendment; (e) the F2 re-credit + the H3-6 query-run.\n- KNOWN RISK: the size of (a)/(b) depends on what the current compilation of context.edn already records — the implementation round's FIRST deliverable is a written inventory of the existing datoms (the honest check), before any code.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The attribution query, run on the final tree, reconstructs 165/165 with call-origin preserved (spot-check: the /1 vs /2 patterns disjoint; a mutated call fails attribution).\n- The gate reads 165/165 credited, zero unexercised; the by-design `:gate-status` assertion PASSES for the first time; H3-6's independent audit and final architect sign-off complete closure.\n- No existing pin regresses; the interim asymmetry documentation and all prior rulings stand.\n{\"type\":\"thread.started\",\"thread_id\":\"01a1aca8-71d2-7ee0-873a-470b3930f664\"}\n{\"type\":\"turn.started\"}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_0\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’ll read the architecture rules and trace the current attribution path, starting with the required inventory of recorded datoms before any code changes. I’ll also check the draft’s review requirements.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"pwd && rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g '*latte*' -g '*gate*' -g '*ruling*' -g '*H3*' -g 'datom.world.md' -g '*linker*' | head -100\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"pwd && rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g '*latte*' -g '*gate*' -g '*ruling*' -g '*H3*' -g 'datom.world.md' -g '*linker*' | head -100\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld\\nnative/jolt-file/probes/gates.clj\\nnative/jolt-file/probes/j4b/fs_gates.clj\\ndocker/oracle/reconcile/probe_aggregates.clj\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.astra-review.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793880000000-architect-h3-1-budget-ruling.kimi-k3.1-pro.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794320000000-architect-cell-ref-two-api-ruling.prompt.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794320000000-architect-cell-ref-two-api-ruling.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-DRAFT.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794330000000-architect-cell-ref-ruling-of-record.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793870000000-architect-h3-1-design-ruling.kimi-k3.1-pro.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794420000000-reviewer-cljrt-r5-ruling.codex.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794100000000-engineer-h3-1-wedge.probe2-gate1.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794040000000-architect-h3-1-continuation-ruling.kimi-k3.1-pro.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794220000000-architect-wedge-findings-gate.kimi-k3.1-pro.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794430000000-reviewer-cljrt-r5-regate.codex.stdout.log\\ndocs/agents/delegate-invocation-reference.md\\nios/Runner/AppDelegate.swift\\nAGENTS.md\\nmacos/Runner/AppDelegate.swift\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md\\ndocs/design/datom.world.md\\ncollab/yin-ucf/1791240000000-architect-d10-lower-inputs-ruling.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\ncollab/yin-ucf/1791206000000-reviewer-d6-engine-gate.glm.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-python/1790982900000-writer-yang-antlr-c4-rulings.claude-opus-5-5.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-python/1791047946000-reviewer-python-c3s2-gate.prompt.md\\ncollab/yang-python/1791114000000-reviewer-c6-reclaim-gate.prompt.md\\ncollab/yang-python/1790982900000-writer-yang-antlr-c4-rulings.prompt.md\\ncoll