 and subagents.           | architecture.                                                       |\ndocs/agents/team.md:136:|                             | subagents, bounded searches, QA spot-checks, verification seats.   |                                                                     |\ndocs/agents/team.md:142:| `gpt-6-astra`               | Most capable end-to-end model. **Best for:** Hardest architecture, | Highest cost. Reserve for the highest-risk or most difficult work   |\ndocs/agents/team.md:158:|                             | moderate-complexity implementation, and subagent fallback.         | specialist work with focused tests and independent review.          |\ndocs/agents/team.md:212:|                             | sessions. **Best for:** Scoped subagent work, bounded searches,    | security sign-offs, or subtle invariant proofs; escalate to         |\ndocs/agents/team.md:233:## Role Selection & Reviewer Independence\ndocs/agents/team.md:240:**Reviewer Independence Rules:**\ndocs/agents/team.md:255:**Reviewer bench and parallel-lane scheduling** (2026-10-26 owner ruling,\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:178:Elaboration emits the CIC vocabulary admitted by Gate S: bound variables, sorts, checked constants with explicit universe instantiations, application, typed lambdas, dependent products, and typed local definitions.\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:205:| Sort with explicit level information | The sort vocabulary frozen by Gate S |\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:214:The first mathematical example is dependent identity: the proposition \\(\\Pi(P:\\mathrm{Prop}).\\,P\\to P\\), with the explicit proof \\(\\lambda(P:\\mathrm{Prop}).\\,\\lambda(h:P).\\,h\\). Its validity comes from Gate S’s predicative theory, not from resemblance to Lean notation.\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:250:## 7. Gate S and the CIC dependency\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:254:Gate S freezes the logical theory before typing implementation. The current plan chooses a minimal predicative core. Impredicative Prop, definitional proof irrelevance, eta, and other Lean-specific extensions cannot enter through notation.\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:260:- Typing-rule implementation and claims that Tao proofs are checked remain gated by Gate S and the CIC milestones.\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:379:| E3 — Checking composition | Candidate medium connected to the CIC checker under pinned configurations | Dependent identity accepted and specified invalid variants rejected, after Gate S and the required CIC milestones |\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:413:- Gate S governs logical meaning.\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:432:| Familiar notation suggests unsupported logical rules | Gate S governs semantics; unsupported conventions are refused |\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:22:The same underlying graph/tuple substrate can represent both, but a Clojure `fn` is not automatically a logically valid CIC lambda term. A checker must interpret the typed logical vocabulary according to a fixed formal specification — one frozen by the specification gate before Phase 1 (Gate S, section 7).\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:116:- The frozen specification version (Gate S).\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:124:**Important subtlety:** The kernel can be written in Clojure without its logic being *Clojure's* semantics. Clojure supplies the implementation; a formal CIC specification supplies the type checking and equality rules — the one fixed by Gate S before Phase 1.\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:160:### Phase 0.5 — Specification gate (Gate S)\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:174:**Gate S is a specification gate, not formal verification.** It freezes a human-reviewed document before typing rules are implemented. Mechanizing the judgments against a model, minimizing trusted primitives, and proving metatheoretic properties remain the long-term confidence work of section 9; nothing in this gate claims them.\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:210:**Acceptance:** The dependent identity proof is accepted and malformed variants are rejected — each judged against exactly the judgments the frozen Gate S specification defines, with rejections citing the judgment violated. This is not yet a complete CIC kernel; acceptance covers the v1 spec fragment and nothing beyond it.\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:334:No finite test suite proves consistency. Gate S freezes the specification before typing is implemented; that is a specification gate, not a proof. Later, and separately, long-term confidence work includes mechanizing the typing/conversion judgments against a model, minimizing trusted primitives, and independent implementations. Until that work exists, the checker's authority rests on the frozen spec, the audited checking configuration of section 5, and the cross-checks above.\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:353:**Definition of done:** The first theorem is checked by CIC logic written in Clojure and executed as compiled Yin code, with no host-side fallback that secretly performs the proof check — judged against the frozen Gate S specification.\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:361:- Universe theory for v1 — resolved 2026-10-28: the minimal predicative core, fixed by the Gate S specification (section 7); Lean-like `Prop` semantics (impredicativity, proof irrelevance, eta, `imax`) are versioned spec extensions at Phase 7.\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:34:| Predicted B/C outcomes and C-lane trace records (§4, §7) | Source-derived from [`context.edn`](context.edn) and the harness's `kernel-rewrite` / `kernel-trace-form` / `kernel-executed-arity?` rules; **not executed** — executing B/C requires harness integration, which is the next round. Nothing here claims B/C agreement has been observed |\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:81:## 2. The executed-arity rule, and what each fixture must trace\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:84:`kernel-executed-arity?` in\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:105:  the direct branch tests `:pass` status, `kernel-executed-arity?`, and\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:108:  `kernel-executed-arity?`. The flag is carried on each result row\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:319:`kernel-executed-arity?` for upstream `/1` seeks\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:682:| R2 `:h3-3-registered-definition-nonsymbol` | `latte-kernel.defenv/registered-definition?` / 1 (guest: bare context call) | `[registered-definition? 2]`, `[registered-definition? 3]` | **No** — `kernel-executed-arity?` for `/1` seeks `[gate-defenv-registered-definition? 1]`; the guest call bypasses the gate wrapper and no bare-name mapping exists (§7) |\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:884:   164/165 exercised, the sole remainder\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_gate.md:4:165/165 closure claim because membership in a static dependency closure did\n;; Slice 8 portability source, adaptation v1, pinned LaTTe 86fca0d1.\n;; A context occupies the def-env argument throughout the adapted kernel.\n;; Read operations retain it; writes return a new context. No ambient defaults:\n;; callers supply both extension bindings at initialization. A fresh allocation\n;; returns [name context]. Runtime closures are constructed by guest evaluation.\n[(defn context-not-empty [x] (if (seq x) x nil))\n (defn context-ffirst [x] (first (first x)))\n (defn context-list-star\n   ([a tail] (cons a tail))\n   ([a b tail] (cons a (cons b tail))))\n (defn lookup-in [coll path]\n   (reduce (fn [coll k] (get coll k)) coll path))\n (defn ->Definition [name params arity parsed-term term-free-vars type opts]\n   {:latte/kind :definition :name name :params params :arity arity\n    :parsed-term parsed-term :term-free-vars term-free-vars :type type :opts opts})\n (defn ->Theorem [name params arity type proof]\n   {:latte/kind :theorem :name name :params params :arity arity :type type :proof proof})\n (defn ->Axiom [name params arity type]\n   {:latte/kind :axiom :name name :params params :arity arity :type type})\n (defn ->Notation [name notation-fn]\n   {:latte/kind :notation :name name :notation-fn notation-fn})\n (defn ->Implicit [name implicit-fn]\n   {:latte/kind :implicit :name name :implicit-fn implicit-fn})\n (defn definition? [v] (= :definition (:latte/kind v)))\n (defn theorem? [v] (= :theorem (:latte/kind v)))\n (defn axiom? [v] (= :axiom (:latte/kind v)))\n (defn notation? [v] (= :notation (:latte/kind v)))\n (defn implicit? [v] (= :implicit (:latte/kind v)))\n (defn latte-definition? [v]\n   (or (definition? v) (theorem? v) (axiom? v) (notation? v) (implicit? v)))\n (defn kernel-context [definitions opacity-policy unfold-implicit]\n   {:definitions definitions\n    :unparser-registry {:fundamental [] :standard [] :user [] :unparsers {}}\n    :opacity-policy opacity-policy :unfold-implicit unfold-implicit :fresh 0})\n (defn local-definitions [kernel] (second (:definitions kernel)))\n (defn register-definition\n   ([kernel rdef] (register-definition kernel rdef false))\n   ([kernel rdef local?]\n    (let [[globals locals] (:definitions kernel)]\n      (assoc kernel :definitions\n             (if local? [globals (assoc locals (:name rdef) rdef)]\n                 [(assoc globals (:name rdef) rdef) locals])))))\n (defn fetch-definition\n   ([kernel dname] (fetch-definition kernel dname false))\n   ([kernel dname local-only?]\n    (let [[globals locals] (:definitions kernel)]\n      (if (symbol? dname)\n        (if-let [ldef (get locals dname)]\n          [:ok ldef]\n          (if local-only?\n            [:ko {:msg \"No such (local) definition\" :def dname}]\n            (if-let [gdef (get globals dname)]\n              [:ok gdef]\n              [:ko {:msg \"No such (global) definition\" :def dname}])))\n        (throw (ex-info \"Cannot fetch definition (please report)\" {:dname dname}))))))\n (defn registered-definition?\n   ([kernel dname] (registered-definition? kernel dname false))\n   ([kernel dname local?] (= :ok (first (fetch-definition kernel dname local?)))))\n (defn qualify-def [kernel dname]\n   (if (= :ok (first (fetch-definition kernel dname)))\n     dname\n     (throw (ex-info \"Cannot qualify symbol: not a known definition\" {:symbol dname}))))\n (defn fresh-name [kernel prefix]\n   [(gensym prefix (:fresh kernel)) (assoc kernel :fresh (+ (:fresh kernel) 1))])\n (defn install-unfold-implicit [kernel unfold-fn]\n   (assoc kernel :unfold-implicit unfold-fn))\n (defn install-opacity-function [kernel opacity-fn]\n   (assoc kernel :opacity-policy opacity-fn))\n (defn missing-opacity-policy [ddef opaque?]\n   (throw (ex-info \"Opacity management function not installed (please report)\" {:weird true})))\n (defn missing-unfold-implicit [kernel ctx implicit-def args]\n   (throw (ex-info \"Implicit unfolding function not installed\" {})))\n (defn definition-opacity [ddef opaque?]\n   (assoc ddef :opts (assoc (:opts ddef) :opaque opaque?)))\n (defn is-opaque? [ddef]\n   (if-let [opts (get ddef :opts)] (get opts :opaque)))\n (defn elab-opacity [kernel names opaque?]\n   (reduce\n     (fn [kernel dname]\n       (let [[status ddef] (fetch-definition kernel dname)]\n         (when (= status :ko)\n           (throw (ex-info \"Cannot qualify symbol: not a known definition\" {:symbol dname})))\n         (when (= (if (is-opaque? ddef) true false) opaque?)\n           (throw (ex-info (if opaque? \":opaque fails: definition is already opaque\"\n                              \":transparent fails: definition is not opaque\") {:name dname})))\n         (let [updated ((:opacity-policy kernel) ddef opaque?)]\n           (register-definition kernel updated (contains? (local-definitions kernel) dname)))))\n     kernel names))\n (defn elab-opaque [kernel names] (elab-opacity kernel names true))\n (defn elab-transparent [kernel names] (elab-opacity kernel names false))\n ;; Preserve the upstream unregister's :user-from-:standard behavior exactly.\n (defn unregister-unparser [kernel ident]\n   (let [registry (:unparser-registry kernel)]\n     (when (not (contains? (:unparsers registry) ident))\n       (throw (ex-info \"No such unparser.\" {:ident ident})))\n     (assoc kernel :unparser-registry\n            {:fundamental (into [] (remove (fn [x] (= x ident)) (:fundamental registry)))\n             :standard (into [] (remove (fn [x] (= x ident)) (:standard registry)))\n             :user (into [] (remove (fn [x] (= x ident)) (:standard registry)))\n             :unparsers (data/dissoc (:unparsers registry) ident)})))\n (defn register-unparser\n   ([kernel ident unparser] (register-unparser kernel :user ident unparser))\n   ([kernel category ident unparser]\n    (let [kernel (if (lookup-in kernel [:unparser-registry :unparsers ident])\n                   (unregister-unparser kernel ident) kernel)\n          registry (:unparser-registry kernel)]\n      (when (not (contains? #{:fundamental :standard :user} category))\n        (throw (ex-info \"Wrong unparser category.\" {:category category})))\n      (assoc kernel :unparser-registry\n             (assoc registry category (conj (get registry category) ident)\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
