{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'executed-arity|H3-6|Gate S|exact mapped' --glob '*.md' --glob '\"'!collab/**'\"' --glob '\"'!orchestrator-logs/**'\"' | head -95; sed -n '2160,2230p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc; sed -n '2950,3040p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc; sed -n '1,135p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ledger.cljc; sed -n '2140,2217p' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc; rg -n 'queue-ast\"'!|queue-rows!'\"' test/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"docs/slice9-h3-1-blocked-fixtures.md:7:- **Production & Gate Source Changes**: **None** (`src/cljc/` untouched; `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj`, `kernel_gate.edn`, `slice9_evidence.edn`, `slice9_verification.edn`, and `:instruction-budget-per-lane 2000000` untouched). One untracked H3-0 resource file omitted from commit `1fd7236be` (`test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_declarations.edn`) is restored so `yang.latte-oracle-test` loads cleanly in fresh worktrees (see §2.4).\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:13920:Done: GLM-5.3 revised the CIC plan for all four Astra findings; Astra confirmed each resolved at plan level and reported no blocking defect. The predicative Prop identity example inhabits U₁ under usual max-Pi rules; Gate S must specify precise rules and admission.\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:13921:Decisions: Gate S and trust composition specified; no implementation, commit, or assertion that gates have passed.\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:13924:Next: Write and review Gate S specification before typing implementation; verify host capability and implement the stated trust and cache contracts.\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:13930:Done: Dispatched a read-only architect review of docs/yin.cic-kernel.md via the cmd lane (qwen/qwen3.8-max, --plan) per the architect.md template. No blocking architectural defect; prior round's four findings (Gate S, runtime trust identity, cache keys, portability evidence) remain resolved. Findings: 3 Medium (resource budget absent from checking-configuration identity :56/:111-116/:263; Gate S freeze list omits recursion/termination policy for transparent defs :166-167 vs :362; freeze enforcement seam unnamed vs resolve-var/require runtime mutability :121-122), 5 Minor (closed open-question :358; src/yin/cic layout should be src/cljc/yin/cic :281-292; stale yang.clojure audit baseline :75; imax in illustrative grammar :142 vs :170; vague interned-node restriction :140), 2 Info (Phase 6 UCF dependency unnamed :261-267; typo :14).\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:13966:Next: Owner decision on the 8 doc deltas (3 High first) — reconciliation is an Architect-level doc edit, not orchestrator-owned. Gate S specification still absent (noted by both this consult and the qwen3.8-max review).\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:14199:Next: yang.tao is a proposed design (no implementation). Prerequisite chains: Gate S spec (docs/design/yin.cic.spec.md still absent — noted by the note itself), the CIC gap slices 3-9 still owed to engineers. Node/Dart lanes for 76f3bce1 still owed on remote hosts before push.\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:34:| Predicted B/C outcomes and C-lane trace records (§4, §7) | Source-derived from [`context.edn`](context.edn) and the harness's `kernel-rewrite` / `kernel-trace-form` / `kernel-executed-arity?` rules; **not executed** — executing B/C requires harness integration, which is the next round. Nothing here claims B/C agreement has been observed |\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:81:## 2. The executed-arity rule, and what each fixture must trace\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:84:`kernel-executed-arity?` in\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:105:  the direct branch tests `:pass` status, `kernel-executed-arity?`, and\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:108:  `kernel-executed-arity?`. The flag is carried on each result row\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:319:`kernel-executed-arity?` for upstream `/1` seeks\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:682:| R2 `:h3-3-registered-definition-nonsymbol` | `latte-kernel.defenv/registered-definition?` / 1 (guest: bare context call) | `[registered-definition? 2]`, `[registered-definition? 3]` | **No** — `kernel-executed-arity?` for `/1` seeks `[gate-defenv-registered-definition? 1]`; the guest call bypasses the gate wrapper and no bare-name mapping exists (§7) |\ndocs/design/yin.cic.spec.md:1:# yin.cic.spec: the Gate S v1 specification of the Yin.CIC checker\ndocs/design/yin.cic.spec.md:3:**Status:** Gate S v1, frozen on review. Specification version string: `yin.cic.spec/v1`.\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:178:Elaboration emits the CIC vocabulary admitted by Gate S: bound variables, sorts, checked constants with explicit universe instantiations, application, typed lambdas, dependent products, and typed local definitions.\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:205:| Sort with explicit level information | The sort vocabulary frozen by Gate S |\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:214:The first mathematical example is dependent identity: the proposition \\(\\Pi(P:\\mathrm{Prop}).\\,P\\to P\\), with the explicit proof \\(\\lambda(P:\\mathrm{Prop}).\\,\\lambda(h:P).\\,h\\). Its validity comes from Gate S’s predicative theory, not from resemblance to Lean notation.\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:250:## 7. Gate S and the CIC dependency\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:254:Gate S freezes the logical theory before typing implementation. The current plan chooses a minimal predicative core. Impredicative Prop, definitional proof irrelevance, eta, and other Lean-specific extensions cannot enter through notation.\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:260:- Typing-rule implementation and claims that Tao proofs are checked remain gated by Gate S and the CIC milestones.\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:379:| E3 — Checking composition | Candidate medium connected to the CIC checker under pinned configurations | Dependent identity accepted and specified invalid variants rejected, after Gate S and the required CIC milestones |\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:413:- Gate S governs logical meaning.\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:432:| Familiar notation suggests unsupported logical rules | Gate S governs semantics; unsupported conventions are refused |\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:22:The same underlying graph/tuple substrate can represent both, but a Clojure `fn` is not automatically a logically valid CIC lambda term. A checker must interpret the typed logical vocabulary according to a fixed formal specification — one frozen by the specification gate before Phase 1 (Gate S, section 7).\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:116:- The frozen specification version (Gate S).\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:124:**Important subtlety:** The kernel can be written in Clojure without its logic being *Clojure's* semantics. Clojure supplies the implementation; a formal CIC specification supplies the type checking and equality rules — the one fixed by Gate S before Phase 1.\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:160:### Phase 0.5 — Specification gate (Gate S)\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:174:**Gate S is a specification gate, not formal verification.** It freezes a human-reviewed document before typing rules are implemented. Mechanizing the judgments against a model, minimizing trusted primitives, and proving metatheoretic properties remain the long-term confidence work of section 9; nothing in this gate claims them.\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:210:**Acceptance:** The dependent identity proof is accepted and malformed variants are rejected — each judged against exactly the judgments the frozen Gate S specification defines, with rejections citing the judgment violated. This is not yet a complete CIC kernel; acceptance covers the v1 spec fragment and nothing beyond it.\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:334:No finite test suite proves consistency. Gate S freezes the specification before typing is implemented; that is a specification gate, not a proof. Later, and separately, long-term confidence work includes mechanizing the typing/conversion judgments against a model, minimizing trusted primitives, and independent implementations. Until that work exists, the checker's authority rests on the frozen spec, the audited checking configuration of section 5, and the cross-checks above.\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:353:**Definition of done:** The first theorem is checked by CIC logic written in Clojure and executed as compiled Yin code, with no host-side fallback that secretly performs the proof check — judged against the frozen Gate S specification.\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:361:- Universe theory for v1 — resolved 2026-10-28: the minimal predicative core, fixed by the Gate S specification (section 7); Lean-like `Prop` semantics (impredicativity, proof irrelevance, eta, `imax`) are versioned spec extensions at Phase 7.\n    form\n    (lower-function-body env macro-env [] [form])))\n\n\n(defn compile-program\n  \"Compile a sequence of Clojure forms into a single Universal AST.\n   Top-level defs compile to explicit yin/def store writes, sequenced via lambda.\n   Top-level defmacros are lowered to ordinary (yin/def name (fn ...)) syntax\n   with :macro? true on the lambda; the encoder turns that flag into a\n   declaration row and the expander, not this compiler, recognizes calls.\n   The three-argument form takes `opts` opting into the argument-vector\n   convention (see `program-env`); without it the output is the native one.\"\n  ([forms] (compile-program forms true))\n  ([forms tail?] (compile-program forms tail? nil))\n  ([forms tail? opts]\n   (run! check-program-symbols! forms)\n   (let [env (program-env forms opts)]\n     ;; A discarded definition must not become a lexical binding in the\n     ;; following definitions. Descriptor entry and body closures both capture\n     ;; their environment: retaining the previous descriptor here doubles its\n     ;; paths at each definition during machine-data validation and UCF lift.\n     (letfn [(discard-result\n               [ast]\n               (if (outcomes? env) (u/then ast (u/lit nil)) ast))\n             (go\n               [forms macro-env]\n               (if (empty? forms)\n                 (compile-literal nil tail?)\n                 (let [head       (cond->> (first forms)\n                                    (outcomes? env) (check-top-level-discard! env macro-env forms)\n                                    (outcomes? env) (lower-top-form env macro-env))\n                       rest-forms (rest forms)]\n                   (cond\n                     ;; defmacro: ordinary (yin/def name (fn ...)) syntax whose\n                     ;; lambda the encoder declares; later forms see the name\n                     ;; only so the native defn lowering can yield to it.\n                     (and (seq? head) (= 'defmacro (first head)))\n                     (let [defined (compile-defmacro (rest head) env false macro-env)]\n                       (cond-> {:type     :application\n                                :operator {:type :lambda, :params ['_]\n                                           :body (go rest-forms\n                                                     (assoc macro-env\n                                                            (strip-symbol-meta (second head))\n                                                            {:type :user}))}\n                                :operands [(discard-result defined)]}\n                         tail? (assoc :tail? true)))\n                     ;; def: sequence with current macro-env\n                     (and (seq? head) (= 'def (first head)))\n                     (let [[_ name value] head\n                           value (cond-> value (outcomes? env) rewrite-ex-info)]\n                       (when (and (outcomes? env)\n                                  (failing? (assoc env ::macro-env macro-env) value))\n                         (reject! \"A def value cannot call guest functions under the outcome model\"\n                                  {:name name}))\n                       (cond-> {:type     :application\n                                :operator {:type :lambda, :params ['_]\n                                           :body (go rest-forms macro-env)}\n                                :operands [(discard-result (compile-def name value env false macro-env))]}\n                         tail? (assoc :tail? true)))\n                     ;; single form\n                     (= 1 (count forms))\n                     (compile-form head tail? (assoc env ::top-level true) macro-env)\n                     ;; sequence\n                     :else\n                     (cond-> {:type     :application\n                              :operator {:type :lambda, :params ['_]\n                                         :body (go rest-forms macro-env)}\n                              :operands [(discard-result\n                                           (compile-form head false\n                                                         (assoc env ::top-level true) macro-env))]}\n                       tail? (assoc :tail? true))))))]\n   of it discharges (the runtime preludes avoid this by keeping every\n   definition at module level); the runtime global namespace is the\n   linked unit's definition table instead, as a Python module body\n   writes its dictionary. Lexically bound names, quoted literals and\n   macro definitions keep their store shape: a macro is not a runtime\n   value.\"\n  [node names bound]\n  (case (:type node)\n    :literal node\n    :vm/current-continuation node\n    :variable (if (and (contains? names (:name node))\n                       (not (contains? bound (:name node))))\n                (u/app (u/v 'clj/global-get) (u/lit (str (:name node))))\n                node)\n    :lambda (assoc node :body (publish-to-globals (:body node) names\n                                                  (into bound (:params node))))\n    :application\n    (if (and (= 'yin/def (get-in node [:operator :name]))\n             (= 2 (count (:operands node)))\n             (= :literal (:type (first (:operands node))))\n             (contains? names (:value (first (:operands node)))))\n      (u/app (u/v 'clj/define-global!)\n             (u/lit (str (:value (first (:operands node)))))\n             (publish-to-globals (second (:operands node)) names bound))\n      (assoc node\n             :operator (publish-to-globals (:operator node) names bound)\n             :operands (mapv #(publish-to-globals % names bound)\n                             (:operands node))))\n    :if (cond-> node\n          (contains? node :test)\n          (assoc :test (publish-to-globals (:test node) names bound))\n          (contains? node :consequent)\n          (assoc :consequent (publish-to-globals (:consequent node) names bound))\n          (contains? node :alternate)\n          (assoc :alternate (publish-to-globals (:alternate node) names bound)))\n    (reduce (fn [n k]\n              (let [v (get n k)]\n                (if (or (and (map? v) (contains? v :type)) (vector? v))\n                  (assoc n k (globals-child v names bound))\n                  n)))\n            node (keys node))))\n\n\n(defn lower-unit\n  \"The `clj/run-main` entry's unit for the top-level source `forms`: one\n   thunk whose call runs the program — `forms` plus the definitions of\n   the guest library they need, compiled unmodified under `unit-opts`\n   and published to the runtime global namespace by\n   `publish-to-globals` — so the helper's export projection can read\n   each top-level definition. The two-argument form also takes the\n   projected `exports` (names the composition already bound in the\n   runtime global namespace): their free reads publish the same way, so\n   the projection is the caller's binding for them. The program's own\n   `yang.call` call sites need no free-name declaration: the convention\n   preamble `compile-program` emits defines those names locally, and\n   the donor publication pins the `yang.call` module for the qualified\n   reads that remain.\"\n  ([forms] (lower-unit forms []))\n  ([forms exports]\n   (let [source (vec forms)\n         library (library-for source)\n         program (compile-program (concat source library) true unit-opts)\n         macros (into #{}\n                      (comp (filter #(and (seq? %) (= 'defmacro (first %))))\n                            (map (comp strip-symbol-meta second)))\n                      (concat source library))\n         names (reduce disj\n                       (into (set (top-level-names source))\n                             (concat (map (comp strip-symbol-meta second) library)\n                                     (map symbol exports)))\n                       macros)]\n     (u/lam [] (publish-to-globals program names #{})))))\n\n\n(defn output\n  \"The `clj/run-main` value as test data.\"\n  [value]\n  (pr-str value))\n(ns yin.vm.ledger\n  \"The ledger: derivation records, ledger events, and naming rows\n   (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` §5.2, §8).\n\n   The hash chain between a tree and the segment lowered from it lives\n   here, not in the code content: neither value carries the other's\n   address (§5.2), so the link is a `:derive` record — a plain map,\n   content-addressed like any other value — published as a ledger event\n   entity, with the tree and the segment named by address.\n\n   A ledger is written over a `dao.space` transactor value. Ledger\n   tx-data is dao.space tx-data (`[:db/add e a v m]` vectors, tempids in\n   `e` and `m`); `transact!` resolves it against the retained history and\n   commits the resolved datoms through the transactor, which owns\n   transaction time and appends them as one atomic record. A naming\n   datom's `m` names its event entity through the metadata-entity path\n   (§8.2): the transactor's tempid resolution replaces an `m` that is a\n   tempid with the event's allocated id.\n\n   Reading is `dao.space.query`: naming rows through the `current` view\n   and its as-of bound (§8.6), provenance walks as joins over event\n   entities, and the raw `m` of a naming row through the `history` view —\n   latest event per `[e a v]` selected with `max ?t` first, its `m`\n   interpreted second, never a hand-written negation (§8.6).\n\n   Scope: the `:derive` record and event. The `:expand` record (§8.4)\n   waits on the v2 expander, the `:publish` record on UCF custody — this\n   namespace mints neither.\"\n  (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.space.index :as index]\n            [dao.space.query :as query]\n            [dao.space.transact :as transact]\n            [dao.space.transactor :as transactor]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.code :as code]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.linearize :as linearize]))\n\n\n(def lowering-profile\n  \"The lowering profile this repository implements, exactly as published\n   in §5.2.1: `\\\"ast-to-bytecode\\\"` over the UCF §7.3.3 stamp\n   `{:yin.code/contract \\\"v4\\\", :yin.k/version 0}`\n   (`vm/semantic-contract`). A `:derive` record\n   carries this map verbatim, and `verify-derivation` recomputes a\n   derivation only when the record's profile is `=` to the profile the\n   consumer implements (§5.2.2 step 2).\"\n  {:yin.lower/profile \"ast-to-bytecode\"\n   :yin.code/contract vm/semantic-contract\n   :yin.k/version 0})\n\n\n(defn derive-record\n  \"§5.2/§8.2's `:derive` record: `output` was computed from `input` by\n   `:yin.vm/lower` under `lowering-profile`. A plain map, so it is\n   content-addressed by `dao.jing/segment-key`, immutable, and\n   verifiable; its own address is what the ledger publishes. The\n   addresses are the only coordinates — the record holds no log-local\n   id, so it is hashed once, when built.\"\n  [input output]\n  {:yin.ledger/op :derive\n   :yin.ledger/input input\n   :yin.ledger/output output\n   :yin.ledger/function :yin.vm/lower\n   :yin.ledger/profile lowering-profile})\n\n\n(defn derive-event\n  \"§8.2's ledger event tx-data for one record: a local entity with the\n   record's fields as attributes and the record's address as one more.\n   `ev` is a tempid — distinct tempids for distinct events, whether in\n   one transaction or several. The profile travels as the structured map,\n   exactly as it appears in the record; no string rendering is defined.\"\n  [ev {:yin.ledger/keys [op input output function profile] :as record}]\n  [[:db/add ev :yin.ledger/op op]\n   [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/input input]\n   [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/output output]\n   [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/function function]\n   [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/profile profile]\n   [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/record (jing/segment-key record)]])\n\n\n(defn derive-lowering\n  \"The `:derive` material for one row-lane lowering, written by\n   `linearize/project-rows`'s caller (§5.2): `project-rows` produces the\n   vector and provenance and knows no ledger; its caller names the two\n   addresses and mints the record. Returns the record, its address, the\n   event tx-data for tempid `ev`, and the addresses the ledger names —\n   `:tree-address` is the row set's root id, `:segment-address` the\n   vector's `(jing/segment-key vector)`.\"\n  [{:keys [root]} {:keys [vector]} ev]\n  (let [segment (jing/segment-key vector)\n        record (derive-record root segment)]\n    {:tree-address root\n     :segment-address segment\n     :record record\n     :record-address (jing/segment-key record)\n     :event (derive-event ev record)}))\n\n\n(defn name-entity\n  \"§8.3's name entity — a local entity carrying `:yin/name` — as tx-data,\n   written once when the entity is created. A later naming assertion or\n   retraction goes through `assert-name`/`retract-name` on the same\n   entity id.\"\n  [name sym]\n  [[:db/add name :yin/name sym]])\n\n\n(defn assert-name\n  \"§8.3's naming row `[t :assert tree-addr name]` as tx-data:\n   `[:db/add name :yin/code tree-addr]` on the name entity\n   (`name-entity` writes its identity datom).\n\n   With an `event` tempid shared with a `derive-event`'s tx-data, the\n   assertion's `m` resolves to the event's allocated id — the provenance\n   link from the naming datom to its event through the metadata-entity\n   path (§8.2). `naming-event` walks it back. Without one, `m` defaults\n   to the `:db/assert` marker.\"\n  ([name tree-address]\n   [[:db/add name :yin/code tree-address]])\n  ([name tree-address event]\n   [[:db/add name :yin/code tree-address event]]))\n\n\n(defn retract-name\n  \"§8.3's naming retraction `[t :retract tree-addr name]` as tx-data: the\n   reserved `:db/retract` marker in `m`. A validity op is expressed by\n   the reserved marker, never by an event (§8.2), so a retraction carries\n   no provenance link; a later assertion of the same address supersedes\n   it under the fold's latest-event-wins (§8.6).\"\n  [name tree-address]\n  [[:db/add name :yin/code tree-address (:db/retract datom/reserved)]])\n\n\n   `[state completion]`, the completion carrying the verified image\n   under `:image` with its obligations beside it, or the refusal under\n   its id. With `scan?` false the link completes at step 4, the image\n   unscanned and no obligations beside it.\"\n  ([state drive format-kw identity contract]\n   (drive-link state drive format-kw identity contract true))\n  ([state drive format-kw identity contract scan?]\n   (let [id [::manifest (:next-fetch state)]\n         request (cond-> {:yin.link/id id,\n                          :yin.link/format format-kw,\n                          :yin.link/contract contract,\n                          :yin.link/identity identity}\n                   (not scan?) (assoc :yin.link/scan? false))\n         [state _] (request-link (update state :next-fetch inc) request)]\n     (loop [state state]\n       (let [r (step (drive state) fetch-budget)]\n         (if-let [c (some (fn [c] (when (= id (:yin.link/id c)) c))\n                          (:completions r))]\n           [(:state r) c]\n           (recur (:state r))))))))\n\n\n(defn- verified-policy-outcome\n  \"The `:verifying` policy's checks once the record's addresses hold\n   (section 8.1): the profile the record names must be the one this\n   linker implements for the format; the manifest's tree is fetched and\n   verified through `ast-format` at steps 2 to 4 and no further -- its\n   obligations are the walker's, scanned only when the tree is the\n   requested image; and the two de Bruijn formats'\n   recomputed identity must equal the record's output. The semantic\n   leg fetches A through the same pipeline and checks tree -> A before\n   a native derivation may name it as input. Returns `[state\n   outcome]`, the outcome `{:derivation r :output o :tree t :trust\n   :verified}` or a refusal.\"\n  [state drive format-kw derivation tree-addr]\n  (let [profile (:yin.ledger/profile derivation)\n        implemented (get implemented-profiles format-kw)]\n    (if (not= profile implemented)\n      [state (refused :unverified-derivation\n                      {:format format-kw, :profile profile,\n                       :implemented implemented, :missing :profile})]\n      (let [[state tc] (drive-link state drive :yin.ast/code tree-addr\n                                   vm/ast-contract false)]\n        (if (refused? tc)\n          [state (refused :unverified-derivation\n                          {:format format-kw, :profile profile,\n                           :missing (:reason tc)})]\n          (let [tree (get-in tc [:image :value])\n                output (:yin.ledger/output derivation)]\n            (if (= :yin.semantic/code format-kw)\n              (let [[state sc] (drive-link state drive :yin.semantic/code output\n                                           vm/semantic-contract false)]\n                (if (refused? sc)\n                  [state (dissoc sc :yin.link/id)]\n                  (if-let [verdict (ledger/verify-derivation\n                                     tree (get-in sc [:image :value]) derivation\n                                     implemented)]\n                    [state (refused :derivation-mismatch\n                                    (assoc verdict :format format-kw))]\n                    [state {:derivation derivation, :output output,\n                            :tree tree, :trust :verified}])))\n              (let [actual (relowered format-kw tree)]\n                (cond\n                  (nil? actual)\n                  [state (refused :unverified-derivation\n                                  {:format format-kw, :profile profile,\n                                   :missing :relowering})]\n\n                  (not= output actual)\n                  [state (refused :derivation-mismatch\n                                  {:format format-kw, :output output,\n                                   :actual actual})]\n\n                  :else [state {:derivation derivation, :output output,\n                                :tree tree, :trust :verified}])))))))))\n\n\n(defn- checked-derivation\n126:(defn queue-ast!\n133:(defn queue-rows!\n135:   row set (§6.1): the row-lane twin of `queue-ast!`.\"\n194:   `queue-ast!`: fresh code, so the composition takes the trusted\n213:      (queue-ast! ast)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"Two issues need correction in the draft. The linker’s derivations describe UAST-to-lowered-code transformations; they do not yet record upstream-to-adapter correspondence. Also, the trace emits only `[function arity]`, with no call-site or argument-origin identity. The query approach remains viable, but the draft needs to specify how those missing links become verifiable evidence before promising 165/165."}}
