{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '28,46p' deps.edn; rg --files /Users/sto/.gitlibs/libs/latte-kernel 2>/dev/null | rg '/defenv.cljc\"'$'\"'; sed -n '1,90p' src/cljc/yin/vm/encoder.cljc; rg -n 'derive-lowering|derive-event|ledger|index|datoms' test/yang/clojure_test.clj test/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"                           :extra-deps {persistent-sorted-set/persistent-sorted-set\n                                        {:mvn/version \"0.3.0\"}}\n                           :main-opts [\"-m\" \"btree-bench\"]}\n           :test {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                  :extra-deps {io.github.cognitect-labs/test-runner\n                               {:git/tag \"v0.5.1\" :git/sha \"dfb30dd\"}\n                               ;; latte-kernel: the LaTTe proof assistant's kernel,\n                               ;; the differential oracle for the yang.clojure gap\n                               ;; program (pinned; MIT; dependency-free)\n                               latte-kernel/latte-kernel\n                               {:git/url \"https://github.com/latte-central/latte-kernel\"\n                                :git/sha \"86fca0d1d054af005cb37395eae04500d45e6279\"}}\n                  ;; The guest recursion limit allows depth that needs more than the\n                  ;; JVM's platform default thread stack (1024 KB on Linux, 2048 KB on\n                  ;; macOS): under 1 MB the guard loses to a JVM StackOverflowError\n                  ;; before it can raise Python's RecursionError, so the same tests\n                  ;; pass on macOS and fail on Linux. 4 MB is twice the measured\n                  ;; macOS-default minimum, leaving headroom as the prelude grows.\n                  ;; Evidence: docs/agents/known-gaps.md (the recursion rows).\n/Users/sto/.gitlibs/libs/latte-kernel/latte-kernel/86fca0d1d054af005cb37395eae04500d45e6279/src/latte_kernel/defenv.cljc\n(ns yin.vm.encoder\n  \"The encoder observer: the `run-on-stream` consumer the composition\n   attaches over the program medium so the semantic VM never sees a map AST\n   or a datom batch (§9.1, `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`).\n\n   It is a stage in its own right, not a loader arm. The composition hands\n   its session the row medium's writer as the consumer; `run-on-stream`\n   hands it each observed program batch; it projects the batch to the\n   canonical row set and forwards it — appends it to the row medium. The\n   evaluator observer the composition attaches to that row medium\n   independently is the next stage: the two never call each other, they\n   communicate through the row medium alone, so compiler and evaluator stay\n   independently attachable (no direct function-to-function coupling\n   without a stream boundary).\n\n   Projection is this boundary's one job (§9.1): the map AST the frontends\n   emit projects directly through `yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`; a\n   `:yin/*` datom batch projects through the explicit datom→row adapter\n   below — §7.1's projection path, for media that carry datoms.\"\n  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.observer :as observer]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]))\n\n\n(defn source-envelope\n  \"Construct the provenance-bearing input value ruled by D4. The admitting\n   composition supplies its program medium's logical identity and mints a\n   fresh batch token before append. `members` is ordered and `root` selects\n   the member the evaluator runs. The constructor validates but never mints:\n   retries of an unaccepted staged append must retain the caller's token.\"\n  ([source-medium batch-token members]\n   (source-envelope source-medium batch-token members 0))\n  ([source-medium batch-token members root]\n   (let [envelope {:yin/source-medium source-medium\n                   :yin/batch-token batch-token\n                   :yin/batch (vec members)\n                   :yin/root root}]\n     ;; Validating the root origin validates the two opaque coordinates too.\n     (when-not (and (integer? root) (<= 0 root) (< root (count members)))\n       (throw (ex-info \"Malformed source batch envelope\"\n                       {:rule :batch-envelope, :envelope envelope})))\n     (vm/source-origin source-medium batch-token root)\n     envelope)))\n\n\n(defn- datom-child-places\n  [get-attr eid]\n  (case (get-attr eid :yin/type)\n    :lambda [[3 (get-attr eid :yin/body)]]\n    :application (concat [[2 (get-attr eid :yin/operator)]]\n                         (map-indexed (fn [i child] [[3 i] child])\n                                      (get-attr eid :yin/operands)))\n    :if [[2 (get-attr eid :yin/test)]\n         [3 (get-attr eid :yin/consequent)]\n         [4 (get-attr eid :yin/alternate)]]\n    :dao.stream.apply/call\n    (map-indexed (fn [i child] [[3 i] child])\n                 (get-attr eid :yin/operands))\n    :stream/put [[2 (get-attr eid :yin/target)]\n                 [3 (get-attr eid :yin/val-node)]]\n    (:stream/cursor :stream/next :stream/close)\n    [[2 (get-attr eid :yin/source)]]\n    :vm/resume [[3 (get-attr eid :yin/val-node)]]\n    []))\n\n\n(defn datom-entity-occurrences\n  \"The §9.1 datom adapter's path/entity relation for one admitted member:\n   `[member-index path entity-id]` once per structural occurrence. Shared\n   entities therefore appear at every path while retaining one entity\n   identity, which is the information U16's harvest catalogue derives from.\n   Cyclic or dangling legacy graphs fail at this boundary.\"\n  [datoms member-index]\n  (let [{:keys [root-id get-attr error]} (vm/index-datoms datoms)]\n    (when error\n      (throw (ex-info \"Cannot adapt a datom batch with a dangling root\" error)))\n    (when (nil? root-id)\n      (throw (ex-info \"Cannot adapt a datom batch with no root\"\n                      {:rule :root})))\n    (letfn [(walk\n              [eid path active]\n              (when (contains? active eid)\n                (throw (ex-info \"Cannot adapt a cyclic datom AST\"\n                                {:rule :cycle, :entity eid, :path path})))\n              (when (nil? (get-attr eid :yin/type))\n                (throw (ex-info \"Cannot adapt a dangling datom AST reference\"\n                                {:rule :dangling-ref, :entity eid, :path path})))\n              (into [[member-index path eid]]\n                    (mapcat (fn [[step child]]\n                              (walk child (conj path step) (conj active eid))))\ntest/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc:129:  (stream/append! (:stream (:observer session)) (vec (vm/ast->datoms ast)))\ntest/yang/clojure_test.clj:55:(deftest indexing-reader-injects-nested-source-positions\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Gate S|exact mapped|H3-6|executed-arity' collab/yang-clojure --glob '*.md' | tail -70; sed -n '115,148p' test/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc; rg -n 'defn queue-program|encode|project-rows' test/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc; rg -n 'attribution|arity|LaTTe|latte' docs/design/yin.cic.spec.md | head -35; sed -n '1,90p' collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-DRAFT.md; rg -n 'defn- kernel-row|initial-context' test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Gate S|exact mapped|H3-6|executed-arity' collab/yang-clojure --glob '*.md' | tail -70; sed -n '115,148p' test/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc; rg -n 'defn queue-program|encode|project-rows' test/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc; rg -n 'attribution|arity|LaTTe|latte' docs/design/yin.cic.spec.md | head -35; sed -n '1,90p' collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-DRAFT.md; rg -n 'defn- kernel-row|initial-context' test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj\"","aggregated_output":"collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-DRAFT.md:3:# RULING DRAFT — attribution-as-query: the executed-arity rule attributes through the unitary record (DRAFT — pending gpt-6-astra review and owner sign-off)\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-DRAFT.md:9:The kernel gate credits an upstream function-arity when an execution trace names it at the exact arity in both C lanes. The matcher keys on NAME+ARITY STRINGS. For `latte-kernel.defenv/registered-definition?`, this fails structurally: the guest's adaptation (context.edn) has arities 2 and 3 (the guest world has no ambient environment), the 2-arg body self-delegates to the 3-arg form, and upstream's `/1` (ambient) and `/2` (explicit) both correspond to guest executions summarized as `[registered-definition? 2]`/`[3]`. The name+arity string destroys the provenance the pipeline preserved. A global \"+1\" trace-table mapping was proposed and REJECTED (arity- and call-origin-unsafe). Result: 164/165 credited, the gate honestly `:blocked` on one arity, H3-6 (which requires 165/165) blocked behind an owner ruling.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-DRAFT.md:20:1. **Attribution is defined as a query over datoms.** The executed-arity rule's satisfying form: a trace attributes to an upstream function-arity when the query — trace → guest callable → its UAST/module datoms → derivation datom → upstream ns/name, plus the declared call-shape correspondence — resolves to exactly that arity. Name+arity string matching is demoted to an optimization, valid only where the query is degenerate (1:1).\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-DRAFT.md:23:4. **Gate S v1's frozen wording gets a versioned attribution amendment.** The executed-arity rule (\"the exact mapped arity in both C lanes\") is satisfied by address/derivation-resolved attribution per rules 1-3; \"mapped\" is defined by the derivation datoms, not by name equality. One specification bump, no other rule changes; admission denominator unchanged.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-DRAFT.md:24:5. **`registered-definition?/1` credits under the query** — the F2 fixture's declared ambient-shape execution, verified entering the mapped body in both C lanes. The gate then reads 165/165 and H3-6 proceeds.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-DRAFT.md:26:7. **H3-6's audit runs the query.** The closure audit reconstructs 165/165 by executing the attribution query over the final tree's datoms — \"by address or by a query over its datoms,\" the invariant's own phrasing.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-DRAFT.md:31:- MUST LAND: (a) the adaptation module's manifest derivation records (naming its upstream); (b) the declared-transform recording for context-upstream-form (or its absorption into the pipeline); (c) the attribution query implementation (dao.space.query/q over module datoms + execution records); (d) the Gate S attribution amendment; (e) the F2 re-credit + the H3-6 query-run.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-DRAFT.md:37:- The gate reads 165/165 credited, zero unexercised; the by-design `:gate-status` assertion PASSES for the first time; H3-6's independent audit and final architect sign-off complete closure.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793770000000-architect-hardening-3-design.gpt-6-astra.prompt.md:12:Read `docs/design/yin.cic.spec.md` (the frozen Gate S v1 spec), `test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_gate.md` (the gate record), `test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_evidence.edn` + `slice9_unexercised.edn` (the refreshed evidence), and `docs/agents/known-gaps.md` rows tagged yang.clojure. The master is at `d5d56ca3` — read the tree in the main checkout `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld` (read-only).\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793770000000-architect-hardening-3-design.gpt-6-astra.prompt.md:32:Standing constraints: the executed-arity rule (both C-lane traces prove the exact arity) may not be weakened; the four declared exception departures and the two recorded upstream failures (`typing-ascription`, `compile-malformed`) stay declared; no shared-runtime (yang.call / yin.vm) changes without an explicit ruling; instruction budgets are finite (see the evidence's instruction-budget-note).\n(defn make-observer-session\n  \"One medium, an observer attached through the ring buffer's own unary\n   attacher, and a VM: the session `run-on-stream` coordinates. Readiness,\n   loading, and running are the ast-walker's own functions.\"\n  ([] (make-observer-session (create-vm) default-capacity))\n  ([vm] (make-observer-session vm default-capacity))\n  ([vm capacity]\n   {:observer (:observer (make-attachment capacity))\n    :consumer vm}))\n\n\n(defn queue-ast!\n  \"Append one AST program batch to the session's program medium.\"\n  [session ast]\n  (stream/append! (:stream (:observer session)) (vec (vm/ast->datoms ast)))\n  session)\n\n\n(defn queue-rows!\n  \"Append one AST program batch to the session's medium as its canonical\n   row set (§6.1): the row-lane twin of `queue-ast!`.\"\n  [session ast]\n  (stream/append! (:stream (:observer session)) (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast))\n  session)\n\n\n(defn make-encoder-session\n  \"The semantic composition at test scale — the topology the REPL runs (§7.1,\n   §9.1): the encoder observer over the program medium, forwarding each\n   projected batch to the row medium, and the evaluator observer the VM\n   attaches to that row medium independently. `:program` takes map-AST or\n   datom batches, `:rows` row sets; `:encoder` and `:evaluator` are the two\n   `run-on-stream` sessions.\"\n  ([vm] (make-encoder-session vm default-capacity))\n17:   (`make-encoder-session`). The VM holds no program stream of its own.\"\n24:            [yin.vm.encoder :as encoder]\n141:(defn make-encoder-session\n143:   §9.1): the encoder observer over the program medium, forwarding each\n146:   datom batches, `:rows` row sets; `:encoder` and `:evaluator` are the two\n148:  ([vm] (make-encoder-session vm default-capacity))\n153:      :encoder {:observer (:observer program), :consumer (:writer rows)}\n158:(defn run-encoder-session\n159:  \"Drive both stages of `make-encoder-session`'s composition in order: the\n160:   encoder projects and forwards every program batch, then the evaluator\n163:   (run-encoder-session session\n164:                        ;; the row medium's only producer is the encoder\n176:                                                               (linearize/project-rows\n180:                                                             (linearize/project-rows rows)))\n185:          :encoder (encoder/forward-on-stream (:encoder session))\n153:`c ∉ E` is Reject (R-CONST, `:unknown-constant`). `|ℓs| ≠ |qs|` is Reject (R-CONST, `:universe-arity`). The substituted type is closed and carries no parameter outside `ps`.\n307:**Example D-f.** `definition twice [u] (pi (sort (param u)) (pi (bvar 0) (bvar 1))) ...` then `const twice [zero, zero]`: Reject (R-CONST, `:universe-arity`).\n389:| F-7 | R-CONST arity, Example D-f | Reject (R-CONST) |\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-05 03:40:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-05 10:40:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n# RULING DRAFT — attribution-as-query: the executed-arity rule attributes through the unitary record (DRAFT — pending gpt-6-astra review and owner sign-off)\n\nProvenance of this draft: the owner's axiom-2/unitarity observation (\"the yang compiler pipeline is a transformation over dao.stream and preserves unitarity\") applied to the last uncredited arity. The owner's Datalog reframing is the ruling's core: the attribution question is a query over datoms, not a string-matching convention. Status: DRAFT — gpt-6-astra reviews before owner sign-off.\n\n## The problem (recorded)\n\nThe kernel gate credits an upstream function-arity when an execution trace names it at the exact arity in both C lanes. The matcher keys on NAME+ARITY STRINGS. For `latte-kernel.defenv/registered-definition?`, this fails structurally: the guest's adaptation (context.edn) has arities 2 and 3 (the guest world has no ambient environment), the 2-arg body self-delegates to the 3-arg form, and upstream's `/1` (ambient) and `/2` (explicit) both correspond to guest executions summarized as `[registered-definition? 2]`/`[3]`. The name+arity string destroys the provenance the pipeline preserved. A global \"+1\" trace-table mapping was proposed and REJECTED (arity- and call-origin-unsafe). Result: 164/165 credited, the gate honestly `:blocked` on one arity, H3-6 (which requires 165/165) blocked behind an owner ruling.\n\n## The architectural facts this ruling is grounded in (verified today)\n\n1. **The linker already records derivations** (yin/vm/linker.cljc §8.1): module manifests carry `:yin.module/derivations` — addresses of derivation records per format (`:yin.semantic/code`, `:yin.debruijn.code`, `:yin.debruijn.register`) with `:yin.module/contracts` revision strings. The derivation-record mechanism exists; it is currently unused for the oracle's adaptation module.\n2. **The adaptation is pipeline data**: `test/yang/latte_oracle/context.edn` is EDN — the guest-side stand-in functions (explicit `kernel` context, outcome tuples, `:latte/kind` maps) compiled through yang.clojure into guest UAST. Its host-side pre-transformation (`context-upstream-form`, a walk/postwalk binding-rewrite) runs OUTSIDE the record as harness code.\n3. **The correspondence is not a datom**: \"this context.edn function adapts `latte_kernel.defenv/registered-definition?`\" lives in the harness's manifest entries and prose — not as a derivation datom.\n4. **The gate's attribution is string matching**: `kernel-entries` (latte_oracle_test.clj:1442) from harness manifest entries + host `top-forms` arities; traces matched by name+arity.\n\n## The ruling (seven rules)\n\n1. **Attribution is defined as a query over datoms.** The executed-arity rule's satisfying form: a trace attributes to an upstream function-arity when the query — trace → guest callable → its UAST/module datoms → derivation datom → upstream ns/name, plus the declared call-shape correspondence — resolves to exactly that arity. Name+arity string matching is demoted to an optimization, valid only where the query is degenerate (1:1).\n2. **The adaptation becomes a stream citizen.** The context module is compiled through the standard linker with a manifest carrying `:yin.module/derivations` naming its upstream (`latte_kernel.defenv`, `latte_kernel.norm`, `latte_kernel.nbe`, `latte_kernel.presyntax`, `latte_kernel.syntax`, `latte_kernel.unparser`) — the correspondence becomes a datom, not prose. The host-side `context-upstream-form` pre-transformation is either recorded as part of the derivation (a declared transform with its own revision) or moved into the pipeline; host-side invisible rewriting of to-be-recorded source is not unitary and ends.\n3. **The ambient correspondence is a declared, disjoint pattern.** A `[registered-definition? 2]`-shape execution with the adapter's ambient empty-context attributes to upstream `/1`; with a caller-supplied context, to upstream `/2`. The patterns are disjoint by argument provenance, which the record carries (the fixture's declared call shape + the stored bodies) — call-origin preserved by construction, the property the withdrawn +1 mapping could not give.\n4. **Gate S v1's frozen wording gets a versioned attribution amendment.** The executed-arity rule (\"the exact mapped arity in both C lanes\") is satisfied by address/derivation-resolved attribution per rules 1-3; \"mapped\" is defined by the derivation datoms, not by name equality. One specification bump, no other rule changes; admission denominator unchanged.\n5. **`registered-definition?/1` credits under the query** — the F2 fixture's declared ambient-shape execution, verified entering the mapped body in both C lanes. The gate then reads 165/165 and H3-6 proceeds.\n6. **No weakening rides along.** The D2 packet machinery, the cell/authentic? vocabulary, the evidence-walk bounds, and the refusal pins are untouched. The query ADDS attribution power; it removes nothing.\n7. **H3-6's audit runs the query.** The closure audit reconstructs 165/165 by executing the attribution query over the final tree's datoms — \"by address or by a query over its datoms,\" the invariant's own phrasing.\n\n## Honest scoping (what exists vs what must land)\n\n- EXISTS: the linker §8.1 derivation-record mechanism; context.edn as pipeline data; the manifest entries naming upstream ns/name; the execution records.\n- MUST LAND: (a) the adaptation module's manifest derivation records (naming its upstream); (b) the declared-transform recording for context-upstream-form (or its absorption into the pipeline); (c) the attribution query implementation (dao.space.query/q over module datoms + execution records); (d) the Gate S attribution amendment; (e) the F2 re-credit + the H3-6 query-run.\n- KNOWN RISK: the size of (a)/(b) depends on what the current compilation of context.edn already records — the implementation round's FIRST deliverable is a written inventory of the existing datoms (the honest check), before any code.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The attribution query, run on the final tree, reconstructs 165/165 with call-origin preserved (spot-check: the /1 vs /2 patterns disjoint; a mutated call fails attribution).\n- The gate reads 165/165 credited, zero unexercised; the by-design `:gate-status` assertion PASSES for the first time; H3-6's independent audit and final architect sign-off complete closure.\n- No existing pin regresses; the interim asymmetry documentation and all prior rulings stand.\n981:(def ^:private initial-context\n987:               {'INITIAL initial-context}\n1004:           (context-bc [] (list 'fetch-definition initial-context ''clojure.core/map)))))\n1006:    (doseq [call [(list 'qualify-def initial-context ''missing)\n1007:                  (list 'fetch-definition initial-context 5)\n1048:                call (list 'let ['k (list 'register-definition initial-context (cons ctor args))]\n1111:        c (context-bc registry-replay (list 'replay-registry initial-context ops))]\n1120:             (context-bc [] (list 'unparse (list 'install-fundamental-unparsers initial-context)\n1125:                       (list 'let ['k0 initial-context\n1134:          c (context-bc [] (walk/postwalk-replace {'K initial-context} guest-call))]\n1179:                   (list 'register-definition initial-context '(->Definition 'd [] 0 'body #{} 'T {}))\n1185:                       (list 'let ['base initial-context\n1199:                       (list 'register-definition initial-context\n1208:                         (list 'let ['result (list 'elab-proof-step initial-context [] [] {} step [])\n1228:                        (list 'let ['k (list 'install-unfold-implicit initial-context 'unfold-host-constants)\n1247:        call (list 'let ['k0 (list 'register-definition initial-context '(->Implicit 'i (fn [k ctx x] x)))\n1259:                       (list 'let ['k0 initial-context\n1286:                             (list 'let ['base initial-context 'offset 4]\n1373:                                               (list 'install-unfold-implicit initial-context 'unfold-host-constants)\n1407:                                (list 'register-definition initial-context '(->Definition 'd [] 0 'body #{} 'T {}))\n1490:                                      initial-context\n1636:  (let [env (if guest? initial-context 'latte-kernel.defenv/empty-env)\n1735:    (list 'unparse (list 'register-unparser initial-context :gate (:extension fixture)) (list 'quote (:term fixture)))\n2220:(defn- kernel-row\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
