['r (list 'latte-kernel.proof/elab-proof 'env ''[[A ✳] [x A]]\\n                                                (list 'quote script))]\\n                                 '[(first r) (:fresh (first (second r))) (:fresh env) (:definitions env)])}\\n            expected [:ok fresh 0 [{} {}]]\\n            compiled (kernel-compile fixture)]\\n        (is (= {:value expected} (:outcome (kernel-b fixture))))\\n        (doseq [lane [:walker :semantic]]\\n          (is (= [:yin.k/ok expected] (:outcome (kernel-c compiled lane))) (str lane))))))\\n  (testing \\\"check-proof keeps the rebuilt context under its own def-env' binding\\\"\\n    ;; Observe the context passed to the final comparison: check-proof's public\\n    ;; result contains only the proof term and type, so it cannot expose :fresh.\\n    (with-redefs [kernel-sources\\n                  (assoc kernel-sources 'gate-norm-beta-eq?\\n                         '(defn gate-norm-beta-eq?\\n                            [def-env ctx t1 t2]\\n                            (= 1 (:fresh def-env))))]\\n      (let [fixture {:call '(latte-kernel.proof/check-proof env '[[A ✳] [x A]] 't 'A '[[:qed x {}]])}\\n            expected [:ok ['x 'A]]\\n            compiled (kernel-compile fixture)]\\n        (is (= {:value expected} (:outcome (kernel-b fixture))))\\n        (doseq [lane [:walker :semantic]]\\n          (is (= [:yin.k/ok expected] (:outcome (kernel-c compiled lane))) (str lane)))))))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private kernel-named-integrations\\n  ;; Only these named integrations may contribute observed transitive calls.\\n  ;; Static dependencies never establish execution of any arity.\\n  #{:unparser-guest-closure :implicit-typed-closure :implicit-throwing-closure\\n    :check-assume-have-discharge :parse-to-normalize-to-type\\n    :fundamental-install-use :unfold-install-use :opacity-install-use})\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private kernel-unexercised-reasons\\n  (edn/read-string (slurp (io/resource \\\"yang/latte_oracle/slice9_unexercised.edn\\\"))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- kernel-exercise-entries\\n  [inventory fixtures results]\\n  (vec\\n    (for [{:keys [symbol arities disposition]} inventory\\n          :when (= :admitted disposition)\\n          n arities\\n          :let [direct (vec (for [[fixture row] (map vector fixtures results)\\n                                  :when (and (= :pass (:status row))\\n                                             (kernel-executed-arity? row symbol n)\\n                                             (= symbol (first (:call fixture)))\\n                                             (= n (count (rest (:call fixture)))))]\\n                              (:id fixture)))\\n                integration (vec (for [row results\\n                                       :when (and (= :pass (:status row))\\n                                                  (contains? kernel-named-integrations (:id row))\\n                                                  (kernel-executed-arity? row symbol n))]\\n                                   (:id row)))]]\\n      {:function-arity [symbol n]\\n       :exercise (cond (seq direct) :direct\\n                       (seq integration) :named-integration\\n                       :else :unexercised)\\n       :direct-fixture-ids direct\\n       :integration-fixture-ids integration\\n       :missing-dependency (when (and (empty? direct) (empty? integration))\\n                             (get kernel-unexercised-reasons [symbol n]\\n                                  \\\"No passing direct fixture or named integration records this exact arity in both C lanes.\\\"))})))\\n\\n\\n(defn- kernel-host-value-deviation\\n  [id a b walker semantic]\\n  ;; Disposition only: keep every original comparison and all four outcomes.\\n  ;; A new failure outside this exact host-value boundary remains a mismatch.\\n  (when (and (contains? #{:elab-after-qed :check-after-qed} id)\\n             (= :completed (:status a) (:status b) (:status walker) (:status semantic))\\n             (= (:outcome a) (:outcome b))\\n             (= (get-in a [:outcome :thrown :data :meta])\\n                {:nonportable-host-value (str (class clojure.core/meta))})\\n             (= \\\"Wrong proof script: proof steps after qed.\\\"\\n                (get-in a [:outcome :thrown :message]))\\n             (every? (fn [c]\\n                       (and (= :yin.k/error (first (:outcome c)))\\n                            (= (get-in a [:outcome :thrown :message])\\n                               (get-in c [:outcome 1 :yin.k/message]))\\n                            (= (dissoc (get-in a [:outcome :thrown :data]) :meta)\\n                               (dissoc (get-in c [:outcome 1 :yin.k/data]) :meta))\\n                            (= (result-shape (dissoc (get-in a [:outcome :thrown :data]) :meta))\\n                               (result-shape (dissoc (get-in c [:outcome 1 :yin.k/data]) :meta)))\\n                            (values/closure? (get-in c [:outcome 1 :yin.k/data :meta]))))\\n                     [walker semantic]))\\n    {:boundary :nonportable-host-value\\n     :upstream-value 'clojure.core/meta\\n     :evidence {:file \\\"slice9_evidence.edn\\\" :fixture-id id\\n                :path [:a :outcome :thrown :data :meta]}\\n     :reason \\\"Upstream embeds a host function in error data; the guest carries its closure. No agreement is claimed.\\\"}))\\n\\n\\n(defn- kernel-upstream-failure\\n  \\\"Disposition for two pinned upstream failures, guarded by the original call\\n   and every observed boundary. A new failure must still block the gate.\\\"\\n  [fixture a b walker semantic]\\n  (let [completed-c? (= :completed (:status walker) (:status semantic))\\n        c (:outcome walker)\\n        same-c? (= c (:outcome semantic))\\n        unbound-message \\\"Pinned upstream declaration has no definition\\\"\\n        unbound-data {:refusal :upstream-unbound :binding 'latte-kernel.typing/type-of-ascribe}\\n        failure\\n        (when (and completed-c? same-c?)\\n          (case (:id fixture)\\n            :typing-ascription\\n            (when (and (= (:call fixture)\\n                          '(latte-kernel.typing/type-of-term env '[[A ✳] [x A]]\\n                                                             '(:latte-kernel.syntax/ascribe x A)))\\n                       (= :blocked (:status a))\\n                       (= \\\"class java.lang.IllegalStateException\\\" (:exception a))\\n                       (= \\\"Attempting to call unbound fn: #'latte-kernel.typing/type-of-ascribe\\\" (:message a))\\n                       (= :completed (:status b))\\n                       (= {:thrown {:message unbound-message :data unbound-data}} (:outcome b))\\n                       (= [:yin.k/error {:yin.k/message unbound-message :yin.k/data unbound-data}] c))\\n              {:boundary :upstream-unbound-definition\\n               :reason \\\"Pinned upstream declares type-of-ascribe without a definition; B/C explicitly refuse that branch.\\\"})\\n            :compile-malformed\\n            (when (and (= (:call fixture) '(latte-kernel.proof/compile-proof '[42]))\\n                       (= :blocked (:status a) (:status b))\\n                       (= \\\"class java.lang.IllegalArgumentException\\\" (:exception a) (:exception b))\\n                       (= \\\"Don't know how to create ISeq from: java.lang.Long\\\" (:message a) (:message b))\\n                       (nil? (:data a)) (nil? (:data b))\\n                       (= [:yin.k/error {:yin.k/message \\\"seq of a non-collection is not supported\\\"\\n                                         :yin.k/data {:value 42}}] c))\\n              {:boundary :upstream-malformed-proof-sequence\\n| jolt ws: a ping after a local close is queued gets no pong (A4 \\\"every ping produces one pong\\\" holds until a local close exists) | pump.jolt:597 (handle-event! :ping guard); wave-1 Architect sign-off P3 | jolt (J6) | documented behaviour, closed by J6e's docstring sentences; J6h cites the archived sign-off | P3 |\\n| yang.php: compile-time default validation is incomplete: `literal-fits?` handles only string, int, bool and null literals; a float default for an `int` property or parameter (`public int $x = 1.5`, `function f(int $x = 1.5)`), arrays and signed literals pass lowering, and `:unknown` is returned whenever a class, object, callable or iterable member exists, so plainly invalid scalar defaults escape; PHP rejects resolved defaults during compilation | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` default checks | yang.php | P4 S6 (classify statically evaluable defaults completely; defer only genuinely unresolved expressions) | P2 |\\n| yang.php: a later rejected by-reference argument leaks an earlier parameter's ownership: with `function f(D &$a, int &$b)`, `$a = new D`, `$b = 'bad'`, catching `f($a, $b)` and then `unset($a)` destroys the object after the next statement (`cED`) instead of before it (`cDE`), because cleanup surrounds only the body and not parameter initialization | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` parameter initialization | yang.php | P4 S6 (cover parameter initialization in the unwinding cleanup so every acquired parameter is released on failure) | P2 |\\n| yang.php: a later rejected by-reference argument leaks an earlier parameter's acquisition: with `function f(D &$a, int &$b)`, `$a = new D`, `$b = 'bad'`, `catch (TypeError $e)`, then `$e = null; unset($a); echo 'E';`, PHP prints `cDE` (the freed trace releases its copy, `unset` drops the last holder) and yang.php prints `cED` (the acquisition of `$a` made before `$b` failed is never released, because cleanup surrounds only the body, not parameter initialization). Without `$e = null` both print `cED`, PHP because the trace holds the argument (corner C30), yang.php because of this leak | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5, restated by the 17.3 ruling of 2026-10-28; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` parameter initialization | yang.php | P4 S6 (cover parameter initialization in the unwinding cleanup so every acquired parameter is released on failure); fixture `:decl-byref-later-rejection-releases-earlier-param` (`#{:p4-s6}`) | P2 |\\n| yang.php C30: exception traces hold no arguments, so an argument rejected at acquisition is released at the throw where PHP keeps it alive until the exception is freed (departure `cDE` vs `cED`) | 17.3 ruling 2026-10-28 on oracle pass 9; design section 17.2 C30 | yang.php | none scheduled; a trace-frames slice | P3 |\\n| yang.php: compile-time default validation was incomplete (a float default for an `int` property or parameter, arrays and signed literals passed lowering, and `:unknown` escaped whenever a class, object, callable or iterable member existed); closed in P4 S6: `init-kind` classifies int, float, string, bool, null, array and signed numeric defaults from the syntax tree, `kind-fits?` holds them to the declared type (an int widens to float; callable members accept strings and arrays), and only a default that names a constant is checked when the object or parameter is installed | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` default checks | yang.php | closed in P4 S6; `compile-errors-are-named-test` entries and fixture `:decl-signed-literal-defaults-accepted` | P2 |\\n| yang.php: a later rejected by-reference argument leaked an earlier parameter's acquisition (`function f(D &$a, int &$b)`, `$a = new D`, `$b = 'bad'`: PHP prints `cDE` once the trace is freed, yang.php printed `cED`); closed in P4 S6: declared parameter types are checked in the body's prologue (`php/check-param-cell!`), inside the cleanup that releases every parameter cell, and a function with a typed parameter always takes the unwinding cleanup path. Without freeing the exception both print `cED` (corner C30) | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5, restated by the 17.3 ruling of 2026-10-28; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` parameter initialization | yang.php | closed in P4 S6; fixture `:decl-byref-later-rejection-releases-earlier-param` (enabled) | P2 |\\n| yang.php: first-class callable syntax (`strlen(...)`, `$o->m(...)`, `A::m(...)`) is a syntax error in the vendored `PhpParser.g4` (an argument list has no bare `...`); `Closure::fromCallable` and the callable forms (`[$o, 'm']`, `'A::m'`, invokable objects) carry the semantics | P4 S6; `antlr/php/PhpParser.g4` `arguments` | yang.php | a grammar patch slice before P4 S10 (php-src selection uses the syntax) | P3 |\\n| yang.php: the `callable` type check (`php/callable?`) sees only public methods, because a type check has no calling scope; a private `[$this, 'm']` callable passed to a `callable` parameter from inside its class is rejected, while calling it directly works; closure rebinding does not warn for the unbind-`$this` and internal-class cases, and `static` is accepted only as a return type (as a parameter or property type it never matches) | P4 S6 | yang.php | P4 S10 (departure fixtures from the object permutation alphabet) | P3 |\\n| yang.php: destructuring by reference into a property (`[&$o->p] = $a`, row 47) is not lowered: the destructuring pattern accepts variable and element targets only; fixture `:obj-row47-destructure-by-ref-into-typed-property` is authored and gated by `:p4-row47` | checkpoint B audit (fixture package); `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` `pattern-of` | yang.php | P4 S7 or a destructuring slice before checkpoint C (the gate lifts when the pattern accepts property targets) | P2 |\\n| yang.php: `exit`/`die` is refused (`:constant`), so row 51 (`exit` inside a destructor proceeds to shutdown, corner C9) cannot run; fixture `:obj-row51-exit-in-destructor` is authored and gated by `:p4-exit` | checkpoint B audit (fixture package); design row 51 | yang.php | P4 S7 (the exit escape and the pending-exception destruct halt) | P2 |\\n| yang.php: a direct write to a typed static property (`A::$s = 'x'`) is not type-checked; only a reference to it is (a source keyed by class and property, row 46), so assignment through the reference is checked and the direct assignment is not | checkpoint B audit (fixture package) | yang.php | P4 S10 (departure fixture from the object permutation alphabet) or earlier if the sprop write path gains a coercion | P3 |\\n| yang.php C37: `__FILE__`/`__DIR__` use the unit's declared relative path (`.` for a root unit), not absolute filesystem paths; fixture `:const-file-dir-raw` records the departure and oracle comparison normalizes `/w/<id>` to `.` | P6 plan v2 R2, design 17.2 C37 | yang.php | retained as a P6 departure | P3 |\\n| yang.php C38: folded include paths resolve at the composition root; PHP's including-script directory and additional `include_path` searches are not modelled | P6 plan v2 R3, design 17.2 C38 | yang.php | composition supplies a literal path map; resolution expansion is a later inclusion slice | P3 |\\n| yang.php C39: `spl_autoload_register(null)` and implicit Composer filesystem resolution are refused; literal Composer class-map data is supported | P6 plan v2 R9, design 17.2 C39 | yang.php | retained as a P6 departure; fixture `default-autoloader-is-refused-test` | P3 |\\n### 8.10 Shared function calling convention\\n\\n**Status: proposed, 2026-10-29; not implemented.** This section specifies\\none invocation convention for every conforming ANTLR-based Yang frontend.\\nExamples are non-exhaustive. Adding a language must not add a case to Yang\\ncore, a language-pair bridge, or an evaluator transition. The words\\n**must**, **must not**, and **may** state requirements on a future\\nconforming implementation, not claims about current code.\\n\\n#### 8.10.1 Decision and scope\\n\\nA Yin function is a Yin function, independent of its source syntax. The\\nVM applies a closure to operands. Source calling semantics are ordinary\\nUAST implementing the call site and callee entry, above the VM.\\n\\nEvery supported source-level callable, after its source-required\\nresolution, exposes `:yang.call/entry`, an ordinary Yin closure taking\\nexactly one invocation record. Invoking it is an ordinary\\n`:application`. No AST tag, lambda slot, instruction, host primitive,\\neffect kind, or source-language test is added to the VM.\\n\\nThis is an internal calling convention, not a transport, another system\\nABI, or a host callback interface. Calls between source languages inside\\none task do not themselves cross a host boundary. Portable I/O and\\ncross-task interaction continue through `dao.stream`.\\n\\n**Transparent calling** means that an admitted callable can be passed,\\nstored, returned, and invoked by any conforming frontend without a\\nhandwritten language-pair wrapper. It does not mean every foreign value\\nsatisfies every source operation. Invocation, name visibility, and value\\ninterpretation are distinct obligations.\\n\\nThe convention covers ordinary source calls and supported callable\\nobjects, including higher-order calls. It does not make macros runtime\\nfunctions, grant JavaScript `[[Construct]]`, turn a generator into an\\nordinary returning function, or add a source feature a frontend refuses.\\nConstructors and asynchronous operations keep their declared source\\nsemantics; ordinary callability does not imply either capability.\\n\\n#### 8.10.2 Callable representation and identity\\n\\nKeep the callable's existing identity and source-specific data. Add the\\n---\\ndescription: Team roster, role routing, and reviewer-independence rules for datom.world\\n---\\n\\n# DATOM.WORLD SOFTWARE ENGINEERING TEAM\\n\\nCanonical guide for the shared roster, role routing, and reviewer independence.\\nThe Lead Engineering Orchestrator owns coordination, authorization, artifact\\nprotocol, and verification in [`orchestrator.md`](./roles/orchestrator.md);\\ndelegate session protocol and CLI recipes live in\\n[`delegate-invocation-reference.md`](./delegate-invocation-reference.md).\\n\\n## Roster\\n\\nThe Orchestrator is deliberately absent from this table; it is the user-facing\\nagent that centrally routes tasks to these roles. Each role name links to its\\ndelegation prompt template under `docs/agents/roles/`. Review is one role\\nbacked by one file, `roles/reviewer.md`, covering adversarial, routine, and\\nsecurity-sign-off review via its Adversarial Review and Consensus Follow-up\\ntemplates — adjust scope and rigor in the brief per flavor, not the file.\\n\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Role                                              | Responsibility                                                                                         |\\n+===================================================+========================================================================================================+\\n| [Architect](roles/architect.md)                   | Axioms, invariants, boundaries, architecture                                                           |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [Compiler & AST](roles/compiler-engineer.md)      | AST, lowering, compile-time macros                                                                     |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [Frontend & Graphics](roles/graphics-engineer.md) | Events, WebGL/WebGPU, canvas, terminal                                                                 |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [QA & Verification](roles/qa-engineer.md)         | TDD, parity, lint, regression                                                                          |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [Review](roles/reviewer.md)                       | Adversarial defect discovery, routine correctness/portability checks, and security capability sign-off |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [Scoped / Subagent](roles/subagent.md)            | Bounded searches, edits, docs, lint                                                                    |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [Storage & Indexing](roles/storage-engineer.md)   | B-trees, indexing, DHT, storage, query                                                                 |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [Stream & Network](roles/stream-engineer.md)      | Streams, framing, concurrency, transports, codecs, RPC                                                 |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [VM Runtime](roles/vm-engineer.md)                | CESK, VMs, continuations, macros, loops                                                                |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\n\\n## Available Subscriptions & Cost Constraints\\n\\nThe Orchestrator routes work using these cost profiles. Flat subscriptions are preferred; metered models are reserved for tasks explicitly requiring them.\\n\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Provider        | Cost Structure                         | Routing Notes                                                                                               |\\n+=================+========================================+=============================================================================================================+\\n| agy             | Flat: Included                         | Treated as a flat subscription. Use freely within budget.                                                   |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| claude          | Inactive / Cancelled                   | Subscription stopped by owner. Do not route tasks here.                                                     |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| claude-api      | Inactive / Cancelled                   | Anthropic API access stopped by owner. Do not route tasks here.                                             |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| codex           | Flat: ChatGPT Plus ($20/mo)            | Strict rate limits. Available for any role (architecture, implementation, review, QA).                      |\\n|                 |                                        | All codex models (astra, sol, terra, luna) draw ONE shared pool: a cut darkens every seat at once, so       |\\n|                 |                                        | sequence codex dispatches rather than fanning them out. Independent parallel capacity is codex | kimi | glm.|\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| deepseek        | Metered: Pay-by-token                  | Flash: $0.14/M in, $0.28/M out. Pro: $0.435/M in, $0.87/M out.                                              |\\n|                 |                                        | Off-peak (10:00-01:00, 04:00-06:00 UTC & wknds) is 50% cheaper.                                             |\\n|                 |                                        | Cache hits are ~50x cheaper. Reserve for necessary work.                                                    |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| glm             | Flat: Pro yearly plan ($672/yr)        | Peak hours: weekdays 14:00-18:00 UTC+8. Schedule large jobs off-peak (50% rate).                            |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| kimi            | Flat: Kimi membership (Plus+)          | Invoke only through the dedicated `kimi` CLI. Monthly quotas apply.                                          |\\n|                 |                                        | Pro unlocks Highspeed and 1M context for K3.x.                                                               |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| mistral         | Flat: Mistral Pro ($20/mo)             | Invoke via `vibe` CLI. Model aliases: mistral-large, mistral-medium-3.5, mistral-4.0, devstral, codestral.   |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| muse            | Meta login / `META_API_KEY`            | Invoke via `muse` CLI; headless form is `muse exec`. Models:                                                |\\n|                 |                                        | `muse-wonder-1.0` (flagship) and `muse-spark-1.3` (fast lane).                                              |\\n|                 |                                        | Check [`routing-status.md`](./routing-status.md) before dispatch (geo-restricted at last relay).            |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\n## Model Strengths and Selection Guide\\n\\nThe following guide details the strengths, weaknesses, and optimal use cases for each model based on their current (late 2026) technical capabilities.\\n\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Model                       | Strengths & When to Use                                            | Weaknesses & When Not to Use                                        |\\n+=============================+====================================================================+=====================================================================+\\n| `claude-fable-5-1`          | Highest capability for long-horizon agentic work, complex research,| Slower inference; resource hog (rate limits); mandatory             |\\n{:verification :slice-9-execution-arity-correction\\n :recorded-at \\\"2026-10-30T05:14:31.739075+00:00\\\"\\n :branch \\\"yang-hardening2\\\"\\n :base-revision \\\"91f276de0e1ddd4b9ffbbb7f7d3f448a3b29b5f0\\\"\\n :gate-status :blocked\\n :program-status :execution-evidence-incomplete\\n :gate-assertion {:form (= :pass (:gate-status evidence)) :passed false\\n                  :reason :admitted-arities-without-execution-evidence}\\n :fixture-count 106\\n :new-fixtures 0\\n :primary-residual-consumers 20\\n :negative-fixture-count 38\\n :counts {:pass 102 :recorded-deviation 2 :recorded-upstream-failure 2 :mismatch 0 :blocked 0}\\n :admitted-arities 165\\n :excluded-arities 10\\n :exercise-counts {:direct 39 :named-integration 106 :unexercised 20}\\n :previously-credited-arities 165\\n :rederived-from-both-C-lanes 145\\n :withdrawn-unsupported-credits 20\\n :credited-paths-verified 360\\n :parse-call-counts {:c-walker {1 0 2 8} :c-semantic {1 0 2 8}}\\n :instruction-budget-per-lane 2000000\\n :instruction-budget-note \\\"The prior uninstrumented budget was 1,000,000; body-entry diagnostic writes require an explicit 2,000,000 bound. The harness reads the trace only after halt.\\\"\\n :recorded-portability-boundaries [:elab-after-qed :check-after-qed]\\n :recorded-upstream-failures [:typing-ascription :compile-malformed]\\n :four-exception-dispositions-unchanged true\\n :known-gaps-correction-append-only true\\n :manifest-dispositions-changed false\\n :production-source-changes false\\n :upstream-source-changes false\\n :checks {\\n  :arity-accounting {:command \\\"clj -Sdeps '{:aliases {:arity-verify {:main-opts [\\\\\\\"/tmp/latte-verify-records.clj\\\\\\\"]}}}' -M:test:arity-verify\\\" :exit 0 :output \\\"slice9_arity_accounting_test.txt\\\" :tests 2 :assertions 22 :failures 0 :errors 0 :old-counter-red-control-failures 4}\\n  :oracle-test {:command \\\"clj -M:test -n yang.latte-oracle-test\\\" :exit 1 :output \\\"slice9_oracle_test.txt\\\" :tests 26 :assertions 1879 :failures 1 :errors 0 :sole-failure :open-gate-pass-assertion}\\n  :regression-test {:command \\\"clj -M:test -n yang.clojure-test -n yin.vm.macro-test -n yang.clojure.stream-eval-test -n yin.vm.store-write-audit-test\\\" :exit 0 :output \\\"slice9_regression_test.txt\\\" :tests 122 :assertions 1338 :failures 0 :errors 0}\\n  :kondo {:command \\\"clj -M:kondo --lint test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj test/yang/clojure_test.clj src/dev/latte_oracle_tables.clj\\\" :exit 0 :output \\\"slice9_kondo.txt\\\" :errors 0 :warnings 0}\\n  :evidence-refresh {:command \\\"clj -Sdeps '{:aliases {:arity-refresh {:main-opts [\\\\\\\"/tmp/latte-refresh.clj\\\\\\\"]}}}' -M:test:arity-refresh\\\" :exit 0 :output \\\"slice9_evidence_refresh.txt\\\" :asserted-gate-status :blocked}\\n  :cljstyle {:command \\\"/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/cljstyle/0.17.642/cljstyle check test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj test/yang/clojure_test.clj\\\" :exit 0}\\n  :whitespace {:command \\\"git diff --check\\\" :exit 0}}\\n :scope \\\"Both C evaluators on the JVM; no new Node/Dart host verification. Gate closure remains denied.\\\"}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793770000000-architect-hardening-3-design.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:39:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_20\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"**Approve hardening-3 as the following engineering plan. Kernel-gate closure remains unapproved.** The baseline is `d5d56ca3`: 100 agreements, two mismatches, two blocked fixtures, two recorded upstream failures, and 107/165 credited arities.\\n\\nThe plan preserves the executed-arity rule, existing exception departures, upstream pin, and admission denominator. It authorizes no changes to `yang.call` or `yin.vm`.\\n\\nTwo findings sharpen the brief:\\n\\n- All three blocked C executions explicitly report **“Guest instruction budget exhausted” at 2,000,000 steps**. This establishes the immediate failure, but does not distinguish legitimate execution cost from repeated work or continuation defects.\\n- The deviation classifier still requires a bare closure at `:meta`; refreshed outcomes contain a §8.10 callable descriptor. Restoring declarations therefore requires a representation-aware classifier repair as well as reliable evidence writing. See [the classifier](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1932).\\n\\nThe family totals are **11 proof, 4 presyntax, 10 normalization, 17 syntax, 5 typing, and 11 environment/unparser/utils arities**. These are ownership partitions of the initial 58, not promises about additional fixtures needed.\\n\\n1. **H3-0 — Restore declared dispositions and establish trustworthy evidence. Land before fixture expansion.**\\n\\n   Scope: the oracle harness, declaration records, evidence writer, accounting regressions, and gate documentation. Preserve declarations as explicit, versioned input to classification; generated evidence must not be their sole source of truth. Recover their provenance from `ea18c8e4^`.\\n\\n   Carrying a declaration forward must **not** mean copying an old successful disposition onto a changed execution. Revalidate the current fixture against its declared boundary. For the two after-qed rows, check the pinned fixture ID and call, completed A/B/C executions, original exception message, script value and collection shape, and the precise host-function/guest-callable boundary. Validate the guest callable using its actual runtime representation before any lossy evidence rendering; a map containing `{:type :closure}` is insufficient.\\n\\n   Preserve every original outcome and agreement flag. Do not make C-lane callable representations equal merely to obtain agreement. A declaration whose guard fails remains declared historically, but its current row is a blocker.\\n\\n   Acceptance:\\n   - `elab-after-qed` and `check-after-qed` regain `:recorded-deviation`; the summary contains their complete records.\\n   - `typing-ascription` and `compile-malformed` retain their exact guarded upstream-failure dispositions.\\n   - All four declared exception departures retain their existing scope and provenance.\\n   - Mutated calls, unrelated IDs, changed payloads or shapes, forged callable representations, and incomplete lanes fail classification.\\n   - Writer round-trip tests preserve declarations, statuses, raw comparisons, and coverage. Ordinary tests remain read-only.\\n   - Refresh the stale gate narrative and verification record: neither 145/165 nor “only 20 remain” describes this baseline.\\n\\n   With no other changes, the expected result is **100 agreements, two deviations, two blocked fixtures, two upstream failures; 107 credited, 58 remaining**. Any different result requires explanation.\\n\\n   Routing: QA/harness engineer, with compiler-engineer review of the callable representation. Independent reviewer from a different model family; architect review of the disposition guards.\\n\\n2. **H3-1 — Diagnose and resolve the two blocked fixtures.**\\n\\n   Retain both original fixtures, IDs, inputs, and expected A/B results. Smaller diagnostic reproductions supplement them.\\n\\n   The first diagnostic packet must contain a four-cell comparison—each fixture on each C evaluator—with:\\n\\n   - Pinned source/compiler/runtime identities, instrumentation mode, configured budget, actual steps, terminal reason, and elapsed time.\\n   - Aggregate execution cost and phase boundaries for loading/setup versus fixture execution. Check whether the observer runner resets its counter across consumer invocations; do not assume the current reported count is a whole-fixture total.\\n   - Bounded snapshots of control position, continuation depth, call frequencies, and the ordered trace tail at exhaustion.\\n   - Instrumented versus uninstrumented results, including the completing semantic run of `check-accepted`.\\n   - A minimal reproduction if repeated admission, invocation, or continuation activity appears.\\n\\n   Collect diagnostics through test-harness facilities. Do not recursively serialize the attached host session or entire closure environments. Partial traces are diagnostic evidence only and contribute **zero** coverage.\\n\\n   I authorize finite diagnostic trials at **2M, 4M, and 8M instructions**, stopping when sufficient evidence is obtained. These are diagnostic configurations, not an automatic change to the acceptance budget. Before raising that budget, return measured completion costs, instrumentation overhead, progress evidence, and a proposed finite bound with justified headroom. Do not add retries that increase fuel until a fixture passes.\\n\\n   If execution progresses normally and terminates, a measured harness-budget revision can be reviewed separately. If the fault is in Clojure lowering, repair it with a focused regression. If evidence implicates `yang.call` or `yin.vm`, return the minimized reproduction for an explicit ruling before modifying shared code.\\n\\n   Acceptance: both original fixtures complete with A/B/C agreement and exact traces under the reviewed finite configuration. Any approved diagnostic-runner changes also demonstrate that exhaustion remains a blocker.\\n\\n   Routing: compiler engineer leads; VM engineer provides diagnosis where needed, without shared-runtime write authority. Architect reviews the root-cause packet and any acceptance-budget change.\\n\\n3. **H3-2 — Close proof elaboration through a small named integration family.**\\n\\n   **Yes: use end-to-end proof fixtures to cover the elab cluster together.** Do not start by manufacturing eleven isolated helper calls.\\n\\n   Begin with the repaired `check-assume-have-discharge` fixture. Its structure already follows the pinned upstream proof tests: declarations, two local definitions with a reference between them, discharge, and final checking. Measure what it actually credits before adding fixtures.\\n\\n   Build the smallest complementary family covering:\\n   - Named `have` with explicit and inferred types, plus anonymous `have`.\\n   - A local definition referenced by another local definition.\\n   - Discharge with nonempty dependency sets, forcing definition abstraction and rewriting of local calls.\\n   - Completion through `qed` and `check-proof`.\\n\\n   This family targets all eleven listed proof arities. Register integration IDs explicitly. Body-entry evidence must show the exact arity in **both** C lanes; static dependencies and successful B/C-only freshness tests confer no differential credit.\\n\\n   Add source-backed refusal companions for meaningful boundaries, such as duplicate local definitions, invalid declared types, and incorrect discharge. Preserve the fresh-supply and immutable-prior-context regressions. In particular, preserve pinned upstream behavior such as its conservative `update-var-deps`; this slice does not “correct” LaTTe.\\n\\n   Acceptance: all eleven proof targets have passing direct or named-integration witnesses, with concrete expected proof/type outcomes and relevant state assertions. If a helper remains unentered, add a minimal corpus-derived helper fixture for that residual. If meaningful state cannot be compared under the existing context adaptation, return the proposed comparison boundary for review rather than discarding fields.\\n\\n   Routing: compiler/proof engineer; QA independently reconstructs the credit set. Review must examine outcome semantics as well as traces.\\n\\n4. **H3-3a — Close residual syntax utilities; H3-3b — close environment, unparser, and utilities.**\\n\\n   These are separately reviewable fixture slices. Their initial target sets are 17 and 11 arities respectively; subtract credits already established by H3-1/H3-2.\\n\\n   | Family | Fixture strategy | Required observations |\\n   |---|---|---|\\n   | Syntax, 17 | Prefer direct entry fixtures for remaining wrappers and overloads. Use named capture/substitution or alpha-normalization integrations for internal helpers when they actually execute. | Alpha-equivalent and distinct terms; capture avoidance; bound/free variables; freshness collisions; binder/readable-term structure; empty/nonempty assumptions. Enter each remaining overload explicitly. |\\n   | Environment, 6 | Direct predicates and `registered-definition?` overloads; explicit-context fixtures for `qualify-def`. | True/false predicates, found/missing definitions, local/global distinction, qualification. No ambient namespace lookup added. |\\n   | Unparser, 1 | Named register → use → unregister → use sequence. | Removal changes subsequent unparsing; the previous immutable B/C context remains usable. Preserve the documented extra-context arity mapping. |\\n   | Utilities, 4 | Direct `map-with`, `vconcat`, `vcons`, and `zip`. | Concrete nonempty outputs, empty/boundary behavior where relevant, and collection shapes. Exercise an admitted guest callable through `map-with`. |\\n\\n   Prefer fixtures from the pinned upstream tests. Hand-built inputs are appropriate for missing overloads and isolation boundaries, provided they are checked against A and have a concrete expected result. Do not manufacture exceptions for total constructors or utilities.\\n\\n   Acceptance: every target still assigned to each sub-slice has a valid witness, existing credits remain supported, and no equality projection is broadened.\\n\\n   Routing: QA/fixture engineer. Escalate actual lowering or adaptation defects to the compiler engineer rather than hiding them in expected outputs.\\n\\n5. **H3-4 — Close residual presyntax and typing.**\\n\\n   Initial targets: four presyntax and five typing arities.\\n\\n   `parse/1` gets a **direct fixture**. The existing poisoned-`parse/1` regression remains: successful execution of `parse/2` must never establish `parse/1`.\\n\\n   Prefer named integrations for the remaining parser helpers: parse a product, an arrow, and an explicitly registered defined term, then inspect the parsed term and type-check it. For typing, use a parameterized definition/application that exercises argument preparation, generalization, reference typing, and application typing. Add direct residual fixtures only where those integrations do not enter the required helper.\\n\\n   Corpus fixtures supply the starting terms and expected structures. Cover relevant invalid applications or argument failures without using the declared `typing-ascription` failure as credit.\\n\\n   Acceptance: all nine initial targets, less earlier credits, are supported by exact passing traces; parser output and inferred types match the pinned oracle. Unknown names, local/global lookup, and argument substitutions retain their existing semantics.\\n\\n   Routing: compiler engineer with QA fixture support and independent semantic review.\\n\\n6. **H3-5 — Close residual normalization.**\\n\\n   Initial targets: ten arities across `norm` and `nbe`.\\n\\n   Use direct fixtures for `beta-reduction/1`, `beta-step/1`, `beta-step/2`, `redex?/1`, and `nbe/evaluation/1` when still uncredited. Use compact named integrations for argument reduction and explicit parameterized-definition instantiation. Treat `norm/instantiate-def/3` and `nbe/instantiate-def/3` as separate targets.\\n\\n   Fixtures must include reducible and irreducible terms, a nonempty argument path, and explicit local/global definitions where applicable. Preserve opacity behavior and the documented upstream `delta-step` local-resolution limitation. Do not introduce ambient-only resolution or repair upstream behavior to simplify agreement.\\n\\n   Acceptance: all remaining normalization targets are credited, expected normal forms and refusal outcomes match A/B/C, and every fixture terminates within its reviewed finite budget.\\n\\n   Routing: compiler/normalization engineer; QA independently audits traces and expected values.\\n\\n7. **H3-6 — Final evidence audit and closure review.**\\n\\n   Regenerate from the final reviewed tree, then run the full oracle in a fresh process. Record the actual source commit, relevant source/configuration digests, upstream pin, host/runtime, budgets, fixture inventory, outcomes, and raw traces. The existing `:base \\\"7dc18478\\\" :working-tree true` field alone is inadequate final provenance.\\n\\n   An independent audit must reconstruct credits from raw traces rather than trusting `:coverage :entries`. It must establish:\\n\\n   - **165/165 admitted arities credited; zero unexercised.**\\n   - Every credited path names a passing fixture and demonstrates the exact mapped arity in both C lanes.\\n   - Zero unexplained mismatches or blocked fixtures.\\n   - Both after-qed deviations and both upstream-failure dispositions remain declared and guarded; none contributes coverage.\\n   - All original fixtures and the twenty established residual-consumer contracts remain exercised.\\n   - Admission remains 155 bindings partitioned 146/9, and 175 candidate arities partitioned 165/10, unless separately amended.\\n\\n   Keep the final `(= :pass (:gate-status evidence))` assertion unchanged. Passing it is necessary for closure; independent review and final architect sign-off complete acceptance.\\n\\n**Ordering and intermediate states.** Land H3-0 first. H3-1 informs the proof integration work; H3-2 precedes residual fixture expansion so later slices work from measured gaps. Independent fixture preparation may overlap, but shared harness changes and evidence refreshes need one integration owner.\\n\\nAfter every slice, refresh evidence and publish newly credited, withdrawn, and remaining arity sets with fixture IDs. Do not promise monotonic credit: any withdrawn witness must be explained. Restoring declarations does not raise the agreement count; resolving the original blockers can restore the original corpus to 102 agreements, but the resulting coverage must be measured.\\n\\nThe gate remains `:blocked` while **any** admitted arity lacks a witness, any unexplained execution failure remains, or a required disposition fails validation. Intermediate slice sign-off is explicitly scoped and does not constitute kernel closure. Do not suppress the nonzero oracle result or describe it as green.\\n\\n**Verification for every engineering slice.** Run focused tests while iterating, then the full oracle at the slice checkpoint. Preserve the missing-lane, unused-arity, poisoned-`parse/1`, disposition-mutation, and existing consumer regressions. After H3-0, any expected remaining failure must be identified by assertion; new failures require diagnosis.\\n\\nCompiler changes additionally require the Clojure compiler, macro, stream-evaluation, and store-write-audit regression suites, including common-call admission, refusal termination, outcome propagation, and tail-depth controls. Run scoped kondo, formatting, and whitespace checks. Follow the repository’s affected-test tri-host gates at the final slice tip; JVM sharded feedback alone is insufficient. The LaTTe differential claim itself remains **both C evaluators on JVM** unless a separate host-oracle expansion is approved. Record Node/Dart verification with its actual scope. See [build and test rules](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/agents/build-n-test.md).\\n\\n**Admission stays closed to incidental change.** The nine excluded bindings comprise eight output/formatting bindings and `nano-time`; `unparser/show-term` has two arities, producing ten excluded candidates.\\n\\nReopening requires either a demonstrated inventory/classification error or an explicitly designed portable capability that removes the stated exclusion reason. Output admission would need an explicit stream and defined formatting observations; clock admission would need an explicit clock input/capability and a defensible differential comparison. Host stdout capture or access to a host clock alone does not establish portability.\\n\\nAny amendment requires separate architect review, exact binding/arity changes, capability and comparison contracts, fixtures, and regenerated inventories. Newly admitted arities enter the denominator immediately. Difficulty, budget exhaustion, apparent non-use, or proximity to 100% never justifies excluding an admitted arity. Changes to frozen Gate S v1 rules or exclusions require their own specification version; this compiler differential closure grants no such change.\\n\\nThis was a read-only design pass. I inspected the current evidence, harness, historical dispositions, and pinned upstream proof tests; I did not rerun the oracle or modify the checkout.\"}}\n2026-11-05 17:03:42 GMT\n2026-11-06 00:03:42 +07\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-11-05 17:03:42 GMT  \nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-06 00:03:42 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**Review verdict: revise before sign-off.** Attribution as a query is architecturally sound. The draft overstates what the existing record preserves and what the linker’s derivation contract supports.\n\n1. **High | Rule 2 — lowering provenance is not adaptation provenance.**  \n   The linker accepts a closed manifest schema whose derivations cover semantic and de Bruijn formats. Its derivation records connect the canonical tree to lowered images; they do not express upstream function-to-adapter correspondence. Verification requires the implemented lowering profile and matching input/output identities. See [manifest validation](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:740) and [derivation verification](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:2217).\n\n   **Correction:** retain that lowering chain and specify the additional adaptation provenance as ordinary, address-linked data, or explicitly propose a reviewed contract extension. Naming upstream namespaces is insufficient: retain upstream revision/source identity, function and arity, adapter identity, transform identity/options, and call-shape correspondence. The dependency inventory must also include `latte_kernel.proof`, which [context assembly](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:894) actually uses.\n\n2. **High | Rules 1 and 3 — call-origin preservation is an obligation, not an established fact.**  \n   The instrumentation emits only `[fname arity]`. It records neither callable address nor call-site/argument origin. The result retains fixture calls, but that alone does not connect a particular body entry to a particular source invocation. See [trace instrumentation](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1795).\n\n   An explicit upstream `/2` call can supply the same empty environment as ambient `/1`. Equal values cannot distinguish their origins. Likewise, a stored but unexecuted ambient call must not borrow a trace produced by an explicit call elsewhere in the fixture.\n\n   **Correction:** require a verifiable connection between the executed entry and its originating call, scoped to the run, lane and exact program/adaptation identities. This may be derived for a demonstrably unambiguous call shape; otherwise additional execution evidence is needed. Missing or ambiguous provenance must yield no credit. Test explicit-empty `/2`, an unexecuted `/1` beside an executed `/2`, mismatched program evidence, and missing-lane evidence. Preserve the distinction between the originating call and `/3` self-delegation.\n\n3. **Medium | Rule 4 — the amendment targets the wrong specification.**  \n   [Gate S v1](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.cic.spec.md:1) specifies the CIC checker’s logical theory. The executed-arity accounting rule belongs to the LaTTe differential gate and H3 closure requirements; [the gate record](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_gate.md:86) describes it.\n\n   **Correction:** version the differential attribution contract and identify its authoritative document. This change does not, on the evidence inspected, require changing `yin.cic.spec/v1`.\n\n4. **High | Rules 5–7 — closure must remain a measured result.**  \n   The new query may expose unsupported attribution among previously credited arities. “Adds attribution power; removes nothing” must not require preserving invalid credits. Nor does a derivation declaration by itself establish semantic equivalence with upstream.\n\n   **Correction:** make F2 credit and 165/165 conditional on regenerated, passing evidence. Preserve every valid witness, report any withdrawn witness, and keep H3-6 blocked until independent reconstruction succeeds. Retain the documented absence of ambient namespace lookup; F2’s missing-name result does not establish general ambient-resolution equivalence.\n\nThe required initial inventory, from source inspection, is:\n\n| Layer | Present now | Missing for the proposed query |\n|---|---|---|\n| Source/adaptation | Pinned upstream source, `context.edn`, host assembly and rewrites | Recorded upstream-to-adapter correspondence and complete transformation provenance |\n| Compilation | `kernel-compile` produces an AST for each fixture | Adaptation manifest and retained attribution links |\n| Walker ingress | AST converted to datoms and appended to a program medium | Attribution joins to upstream source and invocation origin |\n| Semantic ingress | Canonical rows projected into a semantic vector | Recorded adaptation/lowering chain in this harness path |\n| Execution evidence | Ordered body-entry pairs, separate C lanes, outcomes and fixture calls | Address/origin linkage sufficient to distinguish the ambiguous calls |\n| Gate accounting | Manifest-derived denominator and name/arity matching | `dao.space.query/q` attribution and independently reproducible audit inputs |\n\nThe ingress distinction is visible in [the runner](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/clojure_test.clj:2005) and [stream queue helpers](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc:126). These paths do not invoke the module-manifest linker or publish the proposed adaptation derivations.\n\nThe central principle passes review: **derive credit from recorded relationships, require actual body entry in both lanes, and refuse ambiguity.** Unitarity supports retaining those relationships; it cannot recover provenance never recorded.\n\nThis was a read-only architecture review. No files changed or oracle tests ran; 165/165 and closure remain unverified."}}
